2026-09-02 19:44:48 [nitro@ryzen5] pico_enable_stdio_usb(romulon 1) is needed for pico for me to use cdc commands to use usb coms?
2026-09-02 19:44:55 [gemini-3.7-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 gemini-3.7-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.003 560B 6.00s
2026-09-02 19:44:55 [gemini-3.7-flash] Yes, that is correct.
2026-09-02 19:44:55 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-09-02 19:44:55 [gemini-3.7-flash] In your `CMakeLists.txt` file, calling `pico_enable_stdio_usb(romulon 1)` configures the Pico SDK
2026-09-02 19:44:55 [gemini-3.7-flash] to route standard input and output (such as `printf` and `getchar`) over the USB CDC ACM (serial
2026-09-02 19:44:55 [gemini-3.7-flash] COM) interface for your target (`romulon`).
2026-09-02 19:44:55 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-09-02 19:44:55 [gemini-3.7-flash] A few related points to keep in mind:
2026-09-02 19:44:55 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-09-02 19:44:55 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Disabling UART (Optional): By default, standard output also routes to the default
2026-09-02 19:44:55 [gemini-3.7-flash] hardware UART pins. If you only want USB output and wish to disable the UART output, you can add
2026-09-02 19:44:55 [gemini-3.7-flash] `pico_enable_stdio_uart(romulon 0)`.
2026-09-02 19:44:55 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Initialization: In your C/C++ source file, you still need to call `stdio_init_all()` at
2026-09-02 19:44:55 [gemini-3.7-flash] the beginning of your `main()` function to initialize the USB hardware and CDC stack.
2026-09-02 19:44:55 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Direct TinyUSB Usage: If you plan to manage raw USB endpoints directly with the TinyUSB
2026-09-02 19:44:55 [gemini-3.7-flash] library rather than using standard C library I/O, you would configure TinyUSB explicitly instead,
2026-09-02 19:44:55 [gemini-3.7-flash] but for standard serial communication over COM ports, using `pico_enable_stdio_usb` is the standard
2026-09-02 19:44:55 [gemini-3.7-flash] and recommended approach.
2026-09-02 19:44:55 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
