2026-09-02 19:54:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 gemini-3.7-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.008 9.481KB 8.59s
2026-09-02 19:54:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] While there is no single universal standard, several established conventions from logic analyzers,
2026-09-02 19:54:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] in-circuit emulators (ICE), and emulator monitors are widely used for presenting bus traces
2026-09-02 19:54:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] alongside disassembled opcodes.
2026-09-02 19:54:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-09-02 19:54:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] Here are the most common approaches:
2026-09-02 19:54:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-09-02 19:54:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-02 19:54:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-09-02 19:54:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] 1. The Classic Logic Analyzer / ICE Columnar Format
2026-09-02 19:54:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] Historically used by HP, Tektronix, and modern protocol decoders (like Sigrok/PulseView), this
2026-09-02 19:54:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] format keeps every bus cycle on one line and prints the mnemonic only on cycles where an
2026-09-02 19:54:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] instruction fetch occurs:
2026-09-02 19:54:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-09-02 19:54:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-09-02 19:54:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] Cycle   Address   Data   R/W   Status   Disassembly
2026-09-02 19:54:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] ---------------------------------------------------
2026-09-02 19:54:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] 1001    EA8F      18     R     FETCH    CLC
2026-09-02 19:54:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] 1002    EA90      69     R     FETCH    ADC #$01
2026-09-02 19:54:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] 1003    EA91      01     R     DATA
2026-09-02 19:54:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] 1004    EA92      D0     R     FETCH    BNE $EA90
2026-09-02 19:54:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] 1005    EA93      FC     R     DATA
2026-09-02 19:54:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-09-02 19:54:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-09-02 19:54:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Advantage: Preserves exact cycle-by-cycle hardware fidelity while remaining easy to read.
2026-09-02 19:54:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Key Detail: Non-fetch cycles (such as operand bytes, stack operations, or memory
2026-09-02 19:54:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] loads/stores) are left blank in the disassembly column or marked as `DATA` / `OPERAND`.
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] 2. Standard Monitor / Assembler Listing Format
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] Commonly used by 6502 machine-code monitors (such as the Commodore/Apple II built-in monitors and
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] emulators like VICE or Mesen):
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] EA8F  18        CLC
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] EA90  69 01     ADC #$01
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] EA92  D0 FC     BNE $EA90
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] EA94  88        DEY
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] EA95  D0 F8     BNE $EA8F
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Layout: `[Address]  [Hex Bytes (up to 3)]  [Mnemonic] [Operands]`
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Advantage: Very compact and familiar to retrocomputer developers.
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Requirement: The snooper or host parser must buffer multi-byte instructions so all bytes
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] (opcode + operands) appear together on a single line before printing.
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] 3. Structured Trace (JSON-RPC Extension)
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] If you wish to maintain your current JSON-RPC output format while adding disassembled data for a
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] host UI, you can extend the result object with standard disassembly fields:
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] {
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash]   "jsonrpc": "2.0",
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash]   "result": {
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash]     "addr": "EA90",
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash]     "data": "69",
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash]     "rw": 1,
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash]     "stamp": "8f7a",
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash]     "asm": "ADC #$01"
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash]   },
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash]   "id": 200037
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] }
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] Practical Considerations for 6502 Bus Snooping
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] 1. Identifying Instruction Fetches:
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * Hardware (SYNC Pin): If your hardware taps the 6502's `SYNC` pin (pin 7 on a standard
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] NMOS 6502), `SYNC=1` indicates an opcode fetch cycle.
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * Software State Tracking: If `SYNC` is not wired, the snooper or host parser must track
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] the instruction pointer and opcode byte-lengths to know which cycles are opcodes versus
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] data/operands.
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] 2. Read/Write Indication: Standard notation uses `R` or `W` (or `1` / `0`) derived from the
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] R/\overline\textW pin to distinguish memory reads from writes.
2026-09-02 19:54:50 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
