2026-09-04 09:01:43 [gemini-3.8-flash] A few interesting observations from the source, trace log, and the PIO comment:
2026-09-04 09:01:43 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-04 09:01:43 [gemini-3.8-flash] 1. Bus Activity & Target System
2026-09-04 09:01:43 [gemini-3.8-flash] • 6502 Execution Trace: The trace clearly shows an active 6502 cycle. We can see a tight
2026-09-04 09:01:43 [gemini-3.8-flash] loop executing around `EAAE`–`EAB2` (`E8` for `INX`, `D0 FA` for `BNE`, followed by zero-page
2026-09-04 09:01:43 [gemini-3.8-flash] stores and table-driven reads/writes).
2026-09-04 09:01:43 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Software Polling Artifacts: In `sampleRom()`, the Pico is currently polling
2026-09-04 09:01:43 [gemini-3.8-flash] `gpio_get_all()` with a 100 µs sleep (`sleep_us(100)`). On a 1 MHz or 2 MHz 6502 system, individual
2026-09-04 09:01:43 [gemini-3.8-flash] machine cycles are 500 ns to 1 µs long. Consequently, the trace is undersampling—catching
2026-09-04 09:01:43 [gemini-3.8-flash] occasional bus cycles rather than a contiguous cycle-by-cycle trace.
2026-09-04 09:01:43 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Typo in Client/Echo: Notice the client or log parser prints `"Addresss Data CS RW T"`
2026-09-04 09:01:43 [gemini-3.8-flash] with three 's's, whereas the firmware transmits `"A D CS RW T"`.
2026-09-04 09:01:43 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-04 09:01:43 [gemini-3.8-flash] 2. Transition to `snoop.pio`
2026-09-04 09:01:43 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Shifting sampling from the CPU polling loop to the RP2040 PIO state machine is a logical step.
2026-09-04 09:01:43 [gemini-3.8-flash] Software polling is too jittery and prone to missing edges on a bus clocked above a few tens of
2026-09-04 09:01:43 [gemini-3.8-flash] kilohertz.
2026-09-04 09:01:43 [gemini-3.8-flash] • A common challenge with a PIO-based 32-bit GPIO snoop alongside timestamps is FIFO bandwidth and
2026-09-04 09:01:43 [gemini-3.8-flash] instruction budget:
2026-09-04 09:01:43 [gemini-3.8-flash]   * Pushing both 32-bit GPIO data and a 32-bit timestamp per transaction requires either
2026-09-04 09:01:43 [gemini-3.8-flash] interleaving two pushes per sampled edge, chaining a timer state machine via IRQs, or running PIO
2026-09-04 09:01:43 [gemini-3.8-flash] into DMA to write directly into an SRAM ring buffer.
2026-09-04 09:01:43 [gemini-3.8-flash]   * If Phi2 (the 6502 system clock) is available on a pin, sampling on the falling/rising edge of
2026-09-04 09:01:43 [gemini-3.8-flash] Phi2 provides clean, synchronized bus data without needing time-based delta guessing.
2026-09-04 09:01:43 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-04 09:01:43 [gemini-3.8-flash] Whenever you would like to look at the PIO implementation or DMA ring-buffering strategies, just
2026-09-04 09:01:43 [gemini-3.8-flash] let me know.
