2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.135 70.26KB 117.76s
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] Good shape — this is close, but it won't assemble as-is, and there are two runtime traps once it
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] does.
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] 
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] As written
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] 
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] 1. `jmp x--, loop` has no `loop` label — pioasm will reject it. Add the label at the wrap
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] target.
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] 2. X is never initialised. On SM enable it holds whatever the register last had. Seed it
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] once before the wrap target: `mov x, ~null` gives 0xFFFFFFFF and then counts down forever. Note the
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] placement matters — anything before `.wrap_target` runs exactly once at start, which is what you
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] want.
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] 
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] Minimal corrected version:
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] 
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] 
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] ; snoop.pio
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] ; transmit 32 bit gpio with 32 bit timestamp
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] 
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] .program snoop
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] 
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] 	mov	x, ~null		; once: timestamp starts at max
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] .wrap_target
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] loop:
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] 	in	pins, 32
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] 	in	x, 32
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] 	jmp	x--, loop
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] .wrap
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] 
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] 
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] The countdown wraps seamlessly: when X hits 0 the jump is not taken, X decrements to 0xFFFFFFFF,
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] and `.wrap` sends execution back to the top anyway. Timestamps are the pre-decrement value, pushed
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] after the pins word, so the host reads (pins, tick) pairs. I dropped the `side_set` since nothing
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] drives it — put it back only if you want a scope marker.
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] 
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] Traps to know about before wiring it up
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] 
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] - X is a loop-iteration counter, not a timer. Right now every iteration is exactly 3
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] cycles, so tick delta × 3 = cycle delta, and the host can convert using the SM clock. The moment
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] you add CE gating or any branching, keep every path through the loop the same length (pad with `mov
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] y, y`) or the timeline silently distorts.
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] - Rate. 3 cycles/sample at 125 MHz is ~41.7 MS/s, i.e. ~333 MB/s into the RX FIFO. Even
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] with FIFO join (8 words = 4 samples) that's ~192 ns of slack, so DMA must be armed and sustained,
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] and you cannot stream that over CDC — this stays a burst-capture design, same as your current
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] `sampleRom`: DMA into a RAM ring, then stream out afterwards. A clock divider (say 12.5 → 10 MHz SM
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] clock, 3.3 MS/s) is probably plenty for a 1–2 MHz 6502 bus and buys you 10× the capture window for
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] the same RAM. Make the DMA ring a multiple of 2 words so (pins, tick) pairs never split across the
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] wrap.
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] - Stalls corrupt timing. If the RX FIFO ever fills, `push` blocks and X stops decrementing
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] — subsequent timestamps are wrong with no on-device error. After a capture, check the sticky
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] `FDEBUG` RXSTALL bit for the SM and discard/mark the trace if it fired.
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] - Config side still to write: `set_in_pin_base(0)`, `set_in_shift(false, true, 32)` for
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] autopush at 32 bits, `set_fifo_join(PIO_FIFO_JOIN_RX)`, DMA reading `&pio->rxf[sm]` on the SM's RX
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] DREQ, 32-bit transfers, write-ring mode.
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] 
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] One design note: since the timestamp is exact, gating capture on CE (a `jmp pin` skip path,
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] length-matched) would let a small buffer cover long real-time windows — worth considering before
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] you commit to the ungated firehose.
2026-09-04 09:59:39 [kimi-k3] 
