2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.159 73.33KB 154.50s
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] There's no "pins changed" condition in PIO — the `jmp` conditions are only `pin`, `!x`, `!y`,
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] `x!=y`, `x--`, `y--`, `!osre`. The idiom is to keep the previous sample in Y and compare
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] explicitly:
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] 
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] - `mov x, pins` samples the same 32-bit pin field that `in pins, 32` would read (MOV's PINS source
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] uses the IN pin mapping), without touching ISR.
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] - `jmp x!=y, changed` does the compare.
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] - On change, `mov y, x` makes it the new previous.
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] 
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] Since you want deltas rather than absolute timestamps, the trick is to only push on change and let
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] a down-counter say when. Register budget: Y = previous pins, ISR = tick counter, X = sample
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] scratch. Note we avoid `in` entirely (it would clobber the ISR we're using as the tick), and `push`
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] is convenient precisely because it pushes the ISR — the tick is already sitting there.
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] 
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] 
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] ; snoop.pio
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] ; push (tick, pins) on any change of the 32-bit pin field
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] ; host: delta_ticks = prev_tick - cur_tick, time = delta * 6 SM cycles
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] 
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] .program snoop
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] 
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] 	mov	y, pins			; seed prev = now
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] 	mov	isr, ~null		; tick = 0xFFFFFFFF, counts down
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] .wrap_target
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] loop:
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] 	mov	x, pins			; 1 sample
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] 	jmp	x!=y, changed		; 2 compare against previous
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] 	mov	x, isr			; 3 tick out of ISR
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] 	jmp	x--, save		; 4 decrement (wraps through 0 harmlessly)
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] save:
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] 	mov	isr, x			; 5 tick back
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] 	jmp	loop			; 6
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] changed:
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] 	mov	y, x			; new prev
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] 	push				; push tick (ISR)
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] 	mov	isr, y			;
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] 	push				; push pins
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] 	mov	isr, ~null		; reset tick
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] 	jmp	loop
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] .wrap
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] 
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] 
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] 14 instructions, fits easily. The idle loop is exactly 6 cycles per iteration and decrements the
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] tick exactly once per iteration, so on the host:
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] 
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] 
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] delta_cycles = (prev_tick - cur_tick) * 6 + 6
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] 
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] 
2026-09-04 10:03:22 [kimi-k3] The `+ 6` is the constant length of the change path itself; if you don't care about that accuracy,
2026-09-04 10:03:23 [kimi-k3] drop it.
2026-09-04 10:03:23 [kimi-k3] 
2026-09-04 10:03:23 [kimi-k3] Things to know:
2026-09-04 10:03:23 [kimi-k3] 
2026-09-04 10:03:23 [kimi-k3] - FIFO order is (tick, pins). With `PIO_FIFO_JOIN_RX` that's 4 buffered changes; DMA ring
2026-09-04 10:03:23 [kimi-k3] should be a multiple of 2 words so pairs never straddle the wrap.
2026-09-04 10:03:23 [kimi-k3] - Autopush off now — no `in` is used, so shift config only matters for the in-pin base (set
2026-09-04 10:03:23 [kimi-k3] it to 0 to keep your `gpio_get_all` layout).
2026-09-04 10:03:23 [kimi-k3] - Stall caveat: if a `push` blocks on a full FIFO, those cycles aren't counted in the tick,
2026-09-04 10:03:23 [kimi-k3] so deltas under-measure slightly. With DMA draining this is rare at 6502 bus speeds; check `FDEBUG`
2026-09-04 10:03:23 [kimi-k3] RXSTALL after a capture as before.
2026-09-04 10:03:23 [kimi-k3] - Worst case (pins changing every poll) is a change every 6 cycles ≈ 20M changes/s — ~166 MB/s into
2026-09-04 10:03:23 [kimi-k3] the FIFO. That's still burst-capture territory over CDC, but at real bus rates you'll get long
2026-09-04 10:03:23 [kimi-k3] windows: idle time costs zero FIFO space, which is the whole point of this versus the firehose.
2026-09-04 10:03:23 [kimi-k3] 
