2026-09-04 10:12:54 [glm-5.3-flash] RXSTALL lives in the PIO FDEBUG register, one sticky bit per SM, write-one-to-clear. The SM freezes
2026-09-04 10:12:54 [glm-5.3-flash] when its push blocks on a full FIFO and resumes when DMA drains it — so a stall means a gap in the
2026-09-04 10:12:54 [glm-5.3-flash] timeline, not lost samples — but you want to know about it because tick deltas crossing the gap
2026-09-04 10:12:54 [glm-5.3-flash] under-measure. Check-and-clear fits your style as a named function:
2026-09-04 10:12:54 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:12:54 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:12:54 [glm-5.3-flash] bool checkRxStall(PIO pio,uint sm){
2026-09-04 10:12:54 [glm-5.3-flash] 	uint bit=PIO_FDEBUG_RXSTALL_LSB+sm;	// bit 16+sm, one per state machine
2026-09-04 10:12:55 [glm-5.3-flash] 	bool stalled=(pio->fdebug&(1u<<bit))!=0;
2026-09-04 10:12:55 [glm-5.3-flash] 	pio->fdebug=(1u<<bit);	// write-one-to-clear
2026-09-04 10:12:55 [glm-5.3-flash] 	return stalled;
2026-09-04 10:12:55 [glm-5.3-flash] }
2026-09-04 10:12:55 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:12:55 [glm-5.3-flash] bool snoopStalled=false;
2026-09-04 10:12:55 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:12:55 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:12:55 [glm-5.3-flash] Two call sites. Clear it once when arming the capture so a stale bit from a previous run can't
2026-09-04 10:12:55 [glm-5.3-flash] false-positive:
2026-09-04 10:12:55 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:12:55 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:12:55 [glm-5.3-flash] 	checkRxStall(pio0,snoopSM);		// clear before arming
2026-09-04 10:12:55 [glm-5.3-flash] 	dma_channel_start(dmaChan);
2026-09-04 10:12:55 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:12:55 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:12:55 [glm-5.3-flash] and check it when the transfer completes, before you stream the buffer out, so the flag lands in
2026-09-04 10:12:55 [glm-5.3-flash] the same session as the data:
2026-09-04 10:12:55 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:12:55 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:12:55 [glm-5.3-flash] 	snoopStalled=checkRxStall(pio0,snoopSM);
2026-09-04 10:12:55 [glm-5.3-flash] 	if(snoopStalled){
2026-09-04 10:12:55 [glm-5.3-flash] 		rpcSend("stall","1");	// timeline gap: deltas crossing it are short
2026-09-04 10:12:55 [glm-5.3-flash] 	}
2026-09-04 10:12:55 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:12:55 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:12:55 [glm-5.3-flash] Notes:
2026-09-04 10:12:55 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:12:55 [glm-5.3-flash] - `PIO_FDEBUG_RXSTALL_LSB` is 16; the field packs one bit per SM with SM0 at the LSB, hence `16 +
2026-09-04 10:12:55 [glm-5.3-flash] sm`. Same layout on RP2040 and RP2350.
2026-09-04 10:12:55 [glm-5.3-flash] - The bit is sticky until you clear it — the FIFO draining afterwards does not clear it — which is
2026-09-04 10:12:55 [glm-5.3-flash] exactly why a post-capture check works.
2026-09-04 10:12:55 [glm-5.3-flash] - If a capture shows the flag, either discard the trace or keep it but let the host treat any delta
2026-09-04 10:12:55 [glm-5.3-flash] measured across the gap as a lower bound.
