2026-09-04 10:13:24 [nitro@ryzen5] does cdc have any extra checking i can add b4 implementing new pio
2026-09-04 10:14:42 [glm-5.3-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 glm-5.3-flash Z 🪠 0.7° $0.003 78.26KB 78.06s
2026-09-04 10:14:42 [glm-5.3-flash] Yes — TinyUSB's CDC has several checks worth wiring in before you push bursts of samples through
2026-09-04 10:14:42 [glm-5.3-flash] it. In rough order of value for what you're about to do:
2026-09-04 10:14:42 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:14:42 [glm-5.3-flash] 1. DTR — is the port actually open?
2026-09-04 10:14:42 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:14:42 [glm-5.3-flash] Your `connected` flag is set by a `vidbit.set` handshake, but USB knows definitively via DTR.
2026-09-04 10:14:42 [glm-5.3-flash] Writing into a closed port just silently fills the TX buffer. TinyUSB exposes a weak callback that
2026-09-04 10:14:42 [glm-5.3-flash] fires on open/close:
2026-09-04 10:14:42 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:14:42 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:14:42 [glm-5.3-flash] bool hostOpen=false;
2026-09-04 10:14:42 [glm-5.3-flash] bool hostClosed=false;
2026-09-04 10:14:42 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:14:42 [glm-5.3-flash] void tud_cdc_line_state_cb(uint8_t itf,bool dtr,bool rts){
2026-09-04 10:14:42 [glm-5.3-flash] 	hostOpen=dtr;
2026-09-04 10:14:42 [glm-5.3-flash] 	if(!dtr){
2026-09-04 10:14:42 [glm-5.3-flash] 		hostClosed=true;	// host vanished mid-stream: abort capture output
2026-09-04 10:14:42 [glm-5.3-flash] 	}
2026-09-04 10:14:42 [glm-5.3-flash] }
2026-09-04 10:14:42 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:14:42 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:14:42 [glm-5.3-flash] If your `picosdk` wrapper already implements this callback, have it expose the same flags rather
2026-09-04 10:14:42 [glm-5.3-flash] than redefining it. Gate `rpcSend` on `hostOpen` and check `hostClosed` in the sample-stream loop
2026-09-04 10:14:42 [glm-5.3-flash] next to `watchdog_update()`.
2026-09-04 10:14:42 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:14:42 [glm-5.3-flash] 2. TX backpressure
2026-09-04 10:14:42 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:14:42 [glm-5.3-flash] `cdcFlush()` presumably calls `tud_cdc_write_flush()`, which returns the bytes actually moved to
2026-09-04 10:14:42 [glm-5.3-flash] USB — and accepts less than you gave it when the buffer is full. Checking return values gives you a
2026-09-04 10:14:42 [glm-5.3-flash] drop counter:
2026-09-04 10:14:42 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:14:42 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:14:42 [glm-5.3-flash] uint32_t flushShort=0;
2026-09-04 10:14:42 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] void cdcFlushChecked(){
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] 	uint32_t written=tud_cdc_write_flush();
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] 	if(written<tud_cdc_n_write_available(0)){
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] 		flushShort++;
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] 	}
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] }
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] Also useful before streaming a burst: `tud_cdc_n_write_available(0)` tells you how much room is
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] left, so you can wait for drain instead of overwriting. TX buffer size is `CFG_TUD_CDC_TX_BUFSIZE`
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] in the wrapper's config — worth knowing the number, since your ring buffer sizing depends on how
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] fast the link drains.
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] 3. RX overflow
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] If the host floods you while you're busy streaming samples and you don't call `tud_task()` often
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] enough, the RX buffer drops bytes silently — there's no error flag. You can detect the risk
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] condition: buffer completely full means data was almost certainly lost:
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] bool cdcRxOverflow(){
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] 	return tud_cdc_n_available(0)>=CFG_TUD_CDC_RX_BUFSIZE;
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] }
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] Call it after long streaming sections. Your shell loop calls `tud_task()` every iteration, so
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] you're probably fine, but during `sampleRom` output the 10 ms sleeps are where backlog builds.
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] 4. Free integrity check you already have
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] Your sample lines carry sequential ids (`200001`, `200002`, …). Add a continuity check to rpccoms:
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] if `id != lastId + 1`, lines were lost in transport. Zero device code, and it validates the whole
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] chain — exactly what you want proven before blaming the PIO path for gaps.
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] 5. Throughput budget before sizing the PIO ring
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] Worth measuring now: your current `sampleRom` emits ~85-byte JSON lines, 65536 of them, flushing
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] every 8 with `sleep_ms(10)` — that's a floor of ~82 seconds per capture, roughly 70 KB/s. USB FS
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] CDC can do ~1 MB/s, so the sleeps dominate, not the link. A bench method like `vidbit.keys` but
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] echoing a few hundred KB, timed on the host, tells you the real drain rate — and that number sizes
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] the PIO ring buffer and tells you how many samples per flush you can afford before `sleep_ms`
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] becomes unnecessary.
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] Order of work: DTR callback first (it's the one that prevents silent garbage), then the
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] flush-return counter, then the bench. The RX check is nice-to-have.
2026-09-04 10:14:43 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:19:41 [nitro@ryzen5] how do i hook up callback?
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 glm-5.3-flash Z 🪠 0.7° $0.004 83.03KB 143.00s
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] snoop.pio is functionally identical to the version we settled on — just the v0.1 header. Nothing
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] new to review there.
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] The hookup is simpler than most frameworks: TinyUSB device callbacks are weak symbols.
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] There is no registration call — you define a function with the exact signature anywhere in your
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] project and the linker replaces TinyUSB's empty weak default. That gives you two ways in:
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] Option A — poll, zero linkage risk
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] ` tud_cdc_n_connected(0)` is the DTR state — TinyUSB implements it as a read of the
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] line-state flag. Since your loop already calls `tud_task()` constantly, a named poll is the
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] smallest diff:
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] bool hostOpen=false;
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] bool hostClosed=false;
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] void checkHost(){
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] 	bool open=tud_cdc_n_connected(0);
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] 	if(hostOpen && !open){
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] 		hostClosed=true;		// port closed since last poll
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] 	}
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] 	hostOpen=open;
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] }
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] Call `checkHost()` in `runShell` next to `tud_task()`, and once per flush inside the `sampleRom`
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] emit loop (your sleeps are where a close would otherwise go unnoticed). No new symbols, nothing can
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] clash with the cdc wrapper.
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] Option B — the callback itself
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] Define this once, project-wide:
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] volatile bool hostOpen=false;
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] volatile bool hostClosed=false;
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] void tud_cdc_line_state_cb(uint8_t itf,bool dtr,bool rts){
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] 	hostOpen=dtr;
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] 	if(!dtr){
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] 		hostClosed=true;
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] 	}
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] }
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] Three gotchas, in order of how silently they fail:
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] 1. The declaration must be in scope where you define it. The prototype lives in
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] `class/cdc/cdc_device.h`. Your file already calls `tud_task()`, so the device API is evidently
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] visible through `picosdk.h` — if `tud_cdc_n_connected(0)` would compile there, the callback
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] declaration is too. If it isn't, include the header; without it, C++ name mangling produces a
2026-09-04 10:22:05 [glm-5.3-flash] different symbol and your definition quietly overrides nothing — no error, callback just
2026-09-04 10:22:06 [glm-5.3-flash] never fires.
2026-09-04 10:22:06 [glm-5.3-flash] 2. Duplicate definition. If your cdc wrapper (or pico's stdio_usb, if you build with
2026-09-04 10:22:06 [glm-5.3-flash] `pico_enable_stdio_usb`) already defines the callback, you get a duplicate-symbol link error. In
2026-09-04 10:22:06 [glm-5.3-flash] that case don't redefine — route through the existing one. This is also the signal to prefer Option
2026-09-04 10:22:06 [glm-5.3-flash] A.
2026-09-04 10:22:06 [glm-5.3-flash] 3. It only fires on change. DTR is low at boot; it asserts when rpccoms opens COM6
2026-09-04 10:22:06 [glm-5.3-flash] (CreateFile asserts DTR by default). So `hostOpen` starts false and flips on connect. If it never
2026-09-04 10:22:06 [glm-5.3-flash] flips in testing, your host tool isn't asserting DTR — keep the `vidbit.set` handshake and OR the
2026-09-04 10:22:06 [glm-5.3-flash] two together.
2026-09-04 10:22:06 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:22:06 [glm-5.3-flash] It runs in whatever context calls `tud_task()` — your main loop — so plain flag writes are fine, no
2026-09-04 10:22:06 [glm-5.3-flash] ISR constraints. `itf` is the interface number (you have one), `rts` you don't need.
2026-09-04 10:22:06 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:22:06 [glm-5.3-flash] Using the flags (same for either option)
2026-09-04 10:22:06 [glm-5.3-flash] 
