2026-09-04 10:28:21 [glm-5.3-flash] The main options
2026-09-04 10:28:21 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:28:21 [glm-5.3-flash] ╭─────────────────────────┬────────────────┬────────────────────────────────┬──────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-09-04 10:28:21 [glm-5.3-flash] │  Option                 │  Header        │  Formatting                    │  Allocation  │  Notes                                                                                       │
2026-09-04 10:28:21 [glm-5.3-flash] ├─────────────────────────┼────────────────┼────────────────────────────────┼──────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-09-04 10:28:21 [glm-5.3-flash] │  `std::to_string`       │  `<string>`    │  decimal only                  │  yes         │  simplest; no width or hex                                                                   │
2026-09-04 10:28:21 [glm-5.3-flash] │  `snprintf`             │  `<cstdio>`    │  full printf (`%04x`, `%lld`)  │  none        │  you size the buffer; your existing `log`/`hexout` style                                     │
2026-09-04 10:28:21 [glm-5.3-flash] │  `std::to_chars`        │  `<charconv>`  │  base only, no padding         │  none        │  C++17, fastest, locale-free; integer support since GCC 8, so any Pico SDK toolchain has it  │
2026-09-04 10:28:21 [glm-5.3-flash] │  `std::stringstream`    │  `<sstream>`   │  manipulators                  │  several     │  heavyweight; fine for one-offs (`rpcError` already uses one)                                │
2026-09-04 10:28:21 [glm-5.3-flash] │  `std::format` / {fmt}  │  `<format>`    │  python-like                   │  varies      │  C++20; awkward on newlib targets, skip for this project                                     │
2026-09-04 10:28:21 [glm-5.3-flash] │  `itoa`                 │  —             │  —                             │  —           │  non-standard, avoid                                                                         │
2026-09-04 10:28:21 [glm-5.3-flash] ╰─────────────────────────┴────────────────┴────────────────────────────────┴──────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-09-04 10:28:21 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:28:21 [glm-5.3-flash] Details worth knowing
2026-09-04 10:28:21 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:28:21 [glm-5.3-flash] `std::to_string` — one call, returns a ready `std::string`:
2026-09-04 10:28:21 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:28:21 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:28:21 [glm-5.3-flash] std::string idString=std::to_string(id);
2026-09-04 10:28:21 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:28:21 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:28:21 [glm-5.3-flash] Internally it's just `snprintf` into a temporary, so it's fine for occasional strings and hopeless
2026-09-04 10:28:21 [glm-5.3-flash] for hot loops (heap churn).
2026-09-04 10:28:21 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:28:21 [glm-5.3-flash] `snprintf` — the workhorse for embedded, and the only one that does zero-padded hex directly:
2026-09-04 10:28:21 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:28:21 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:28:21 [glm-5.3-flash] char buffer[12];
2026-09-04 10:28:21 [glm-5.3-flash] int len=snprintf(buffer,sizeof(buffer),"%04x",value);
2026-09-04 10:28:21 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:28:21 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:28:21 [glm-5.3-flash] Buffer sizing rule: 12 chars covers any 32-bit int (`"-2147483648"` + NUL), 21 covers int64. The
2026-09-04 10:28:21 [glm-5.3-flash] return value is the length it wanted — if it exceeds `sizeof(buffer)`, output was
2026-09-04 10:28:22 [glm-5.3-flash] truncated. This is what `pinStamp` should use if you want fixed-width fields; it matches the
2026-09-04 10:28:22 [glm-5.3-flash] `hexout` pattern you already have.
2026-09-04 10:28:22 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:28:22 [glm-5.3-flash] `std::to_chars` — fastest and allocation-free, but deliberately bare:
2026-09-04 10:28:22 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:28:22 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:28:22 [glm-5.3-flash] std::string decString(int value){
2026-09-04 10:28:22 [glm-5.3-flash] 	char buffer[12];
2026-09-04 10:28:22 [glm-5.3-flash] 	char *end=std::to_chars(buffer,buffer+sizeof(buffer),value).ptr;
2026-09-04 10:28:22 [glm-5.3-flash] 	return std::string(buffer,end);
2026-09-04 10:28:22 [glm-5.3-flash] }
2026-09-04 10:28:22 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:28:22 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:28:22 [glm-5.3-flash] Two caveats: the result is not NUL-terminated (`.ptr` marks the end), and there's no
2026-09-04 10:28:22 [glm-5.3-flash] zero-padding — no `std::to_chars` equivalent of `%04x`. So for your fixed-width hex fields,
2026-09-04 10:28:22 [glm-5.3-flash] `snprintf` is actually less code despite being slower. Check `.ec` for `std::errc::value_too_large`
2026-09-04 10:28:22 [glm-5.3-flash] if the buffer might be undersized.
2026-09-04 10:28:22 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:28:22 [glm-5.3-flash] Mapping to romulon
2026-09-04 10:28:22 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:28:22 [glm-5.3-flash] - One-off JSON building (`rpcSend` id, error strings): `std::to_string` — clarity wins, not
2026-09-04 10:28:22 [glm-5.3-flash] a hot path.
2026-09-04 10:28:22 [glm-5.3-flash] - Inside `sampleRom` / `pinStamp` — anything per-sample: stay with `snprintf` into stack
2026-09-04 10:28:22 [glm-5.3-flash] buffers, then construct the `std::string` once at the end. `std::to_string` per field means
2026-09-04 10:28:22 [glm-5.3-flash] multiple allocations per 85-byte line at your stream rates.
2026-09-04 10:28:22 [glm-5.3-flash] - `std::stringstream` — keep it in `updateRPC`-level code; never per-sample.
2026-09-04 10:28:22 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:28:22 [glm-5.3-flash] If you want one reusable helper rather than reaching for the right tool each time, the `decString`
2026-09-04 10:28:22 [glm-5.3-flash] wrapper above plus a matching `hexString(value,width)` covers most needs — but honestly, `snprintf`
2026-09-04 10:28:22 [glm-5.3-flash] alone covers everything in this codebase including the padding cases.
2026-09-04 10:28:22 [glm-5.3-flash] 
