2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] cdcReceiveCallback: three problems, one restructure
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] 1. Context. With pico_stdio_usb linked, the chars-available callback fires from the SDK's
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] USB service path — i.e. interrupt context. Your spin lock is the right primitive for that, but
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] `lines.push_back(std::string(...))` mallocs inside an IRQ, which pico's allocator doesn't guarantee
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] against main-loop malloc interruption.
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] 2. Re-fire gap. The loop caps at 4096 chars, but the callback only fires when chars
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] become available. If a burst exceeds the cap, the remainder sits unread and no new callback
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] comes until the next byte arrives — a stall that looks like a lost line.
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] 3. Buffer-full re-entry. `cdcCharCount==4096` breaks, but nothing resets it; the next
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] callback executes `cdcBuffer[cdcCharCount++]` and writes past the array.
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] All three disappear with the same move — callback as flag, drain in the main loop where malloc is
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] safe:
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] volatile bool cdcCharsReady=false;
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] void cdcReceiveCallback(void *user){
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] 	cdcCharsReady=true;
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] }
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] void pollCDCLines(){
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] 	if(!cdcCharsReady){
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] 		return;
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] 	}
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] 	cdcCharsReady=false;
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] 	int c=getchar_timeout_us(0);
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] 	while(c!=PICO_ERROR_TIMEOUT){
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] 		// accumulate as before, with:
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] 		if(cdcCharCount==4096){
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] 			cdcCharCount=0;		// discard oversized line, don't poison the buffer
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] 		}
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] 		...
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] 	}
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] }
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] Call `pollCDCLines()` at the top of `updateRPC`, and `line_lock` can go entirely — one context
2026-09-04 10:38:45 [glm-5.3-flash] touches `lines`.
