2026-09-04 10:51:10 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:51:10 [glm-5.3-flash] bool setTime(int64_t seconds){
2026-09-04 10:51:10 [glm-5.3-flash] 	timespec ts;
2026-09-04 10:51:10 [glm-5.3-flash] 	ts.tv_sec=(time_t)seconds;
2026-09-04 10:51:10 [glm-5.3-flash] 	ts.tv_nsec=0;
2026-09-04 10:51:10 [glm-5.3-flash] 	return aon_timer_start(&ts);
2026-09-04 10:51:11 [glm-5.3-flash] }
2026-09-04 10:51:11 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:51:11 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:51:11 [glm-5.3-flash] Now romulon's `rtc.set` handler gets a meaningful `success` to report, and both ifdef branches
2026-09-04 10:51:11 [glm-5.3-flash] agree on the type. Note the cast — `tv_sec` is 32-bit on this toolchain, so int64 seconds truncate
2026-09-04 10:51:11 [glm-5.3-flash] there; fine until 2038, but worth knowing the limit exists.
2026-09-04 10:51:11 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:51:11 [glm-5.3-flash] • 2. It gates `wallTime()` on RP2350.* The AON timer starts from zero at power-on (nothing
2026-09-04 10:51:11 [glm-5.3-flash] persists without a battery), and `aon_timer_get_time` returns false until the timer has been
2026-09-04 10:51:11 [glm-5.3-flash] started — so your `wallTime()` returns `""` from boot until the host sends `rtc.set`. With
2026-09-04 10:51:11 [glm-5.3-flash] `setTime` currently stubbed at the call site (`bool success=false;//setTime(t)`), that's the actual
2026-09-04 10:51:11 [glm-5.3-flash] chain behind the `-32501` in trace1, and it'll also be why any wall-time stamping stays empty.
2026-09-04 10:51:11 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-09-04 10:51:11 [glm-5.3-flash] Failure semantics in practice: `start` on RP2350 can only really fail if the always-on clock domain
2026-09-04 10:51:11 [glm-5.3-flash] can't be enabled — not something you'll hit at runtime. The false returns you'll actually encounter
2026-09-04 10:51:11 [glm-5.3-flash] are `get_time`/`stop` on a timer that was never started. So treat the bool as plumbing, not as an
2026-09-04 10:51:11 [glm-5.3-flash] error path needing recovery.
