2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.059 173.5KB 7.26s
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] • Explanation:*
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] What `transfer_count` actually means
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] On the RP2040, when you start a DMA transfer with:
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] dma_channel_configure(..., transfer_count, true);
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] the hardware loads the value you passed into the `transfer_count` register.
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] • This register counts downwards*, not upwards.
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] - It starts at the number of transfers you requested.
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] - Every time the DMA successfully moves one item (in your case, one 32-bit word), it decrements
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] `transfer_count` by 1.
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] - When the transfer finishes, `transfer_count` reaches 0.
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] In your specific case
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] You are doing this:
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] dma_channel_configure(..., max_samples * 2, true);
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] So you are requesting `max_samples * 2` transfers (because each captured bus event produces two
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] words: `tick` + `pins`).
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] This means:
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] - At the start: `transfer_count = 8192` (for MAX_SAMPLES=4096)
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] - While running: it slowly counts down
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] - When finished: `transfer_count = 0`
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] The function you showed
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] size_t snoopCount() {
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1]     if (snoop_dma_chan < 0) return 0;
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1]     return dma_channel_hw_addr(snoop_dma_chan)->transfer_count / 2;
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] }
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] The comment `// transfer_count words` is just a reminder that the value is measured in 32-bit
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] words (not bytes, not samples).
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] Dividing by 2 is an attempt to convert back from "words" to "samples" (since 2 words = 1 sample
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] pair).
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] The problem
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] Because `transfer_count` counts down, this function returns:
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] - While the DMA is still running → the number of remaining samples (not useful)
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] - After the DMA has finished → 0
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] So once your capture completes (which your code waits for with `while (!isBusCaptureComplete())`),
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] calling this function will almost always return 0.
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:38:22 [grok-build-0.1] This is why you're likely seeing either no output or very few lines from the sampler.
