2026-09-04 13:49:27 [grok-build-0.1] • Yes*, the order is currently correct.
2026-09-04 13:49:27 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:49:28 [grok-build-0.1] Current situation:
2026-09-04 13:49:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:49:28 [grok-build-0.1] In the PIO program (`snoop.pio`), when a change is detected, it does this:
2026-09-04 13:49:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:49:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:49:28 [grok-build-0.1] push      ; pushes tick first
2026-09-04 13:49:28 [grok-build-0.1] push      ; pushes pins second
2026-09-04 13:49:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:49:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:49:28 [grok-build-0.1] So every pair that comes out of the PIO FIFO is in this order:
2026-09-04 13:49:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:49:28 [grok-build-0.1] - First word → tick (time value)
2026-09-04 13:49:28 [grok-build-0.1] - Second word → pins
2026-09-04 13:49:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:49:28 [grok-build-0.1] Your code respects this:
2026-09-04 13:49:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:49:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:49:28 [grok-build-0.1] uint32_t tick  = captureBuffer[i * 2];      // even = tick
2026-09-04 13:49:28 [grok-build-0.1] uint32_t pins  = captureBuffer[i * 2 + 1];  // odd  = pins
2026-09-04 13:49:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:49:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:49:28 [grok-build-0.1] This matches the order the PIO is pushing.
2026-09-04 13:49:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:49:28 [grok-build-0.1] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-04 13:49:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:49:28 [grok-build-0.1] Important caveat:
2026-09-04 13:49:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:49:28 [grok-build-0.1] Even though the order is correct, the `tick` value itself is not a real timestamp.
2026-09-04 13:49:28 [grok-build-0.1] It is a countdown value from the PIO (it starts at 0xFFFFFFFF and counts down between changes).
2026-09-04 13:49:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:49:28 [grok-build-0.1] So while the pairing is right, the actual time values you're getting are still not useful for
2026-09-04 13:49:28 [grok-build-0.1] timing analysis.
2026-09-04 13:49:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 13:49:28 [grok-build-0.1] Would you like to look at fixing the timestamp quality next, or is there something else about the
2026-09-04 13:49:28 [grok-build-0.1] ordering you're unsure about?
