2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] • Answer:*
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] The current reset is intentional in the PIO program. Every time the bus changes, it does:
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] mov     isr, ~null     ; reset tick
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] This is why the timestamp value "resets" on every event (it starts counting down again from
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] `0xFFFFFFFF` after each change).
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] What you want
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] You want a monotonic / continuously increasing (or at least non-resetting) timestamp across
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] the whole capture.
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] Recommended Solution
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] There are two good ways to do this:
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] Option 1: Accumulate on the C++ side (Best)
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] Keep the PIO as it is (it gives you deltas), but build an absolute timestamp in software.
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] In `sampleRomPio()`, do something like this:
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] uint64_t absoluteTime = 0;
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] uint32_t lastTick = 0xFFFFFFFF;
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] for (size_t i = 0; i < count; i++) {
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1]     uint32_t tick = captureBuffer[i * 2];
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1]     uint32_t pins = captureBuffer[i * 2 + 1];
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1]     // Calculate delta since last event (because it counts down)
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1]     uint32_t delta = lastTick - tick;
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1]     absoluteTime += delta;
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1]     lastTick = tick;
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1]     // Use absoluteTime instead of the raw tick
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1]     rpcSend("sample", pinStamp(absoluteTime, pins));
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1]     ...
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] }
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] This gives you a steadily increasing timestamp.
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] Option 2: Stop resetting in the PIO (Simpler but less accurate)
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] If you want the raw `t` value coming out of the PIO to never reset, modify the PIO so it
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] doesn't reset the counter on every change.
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] In `snoop.pio`, change the `changed` section from this:
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1] changed:
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1]     mov     y, x
2026-09-04 14:50:35 [grok-build-0.1]     push
2026-09-04 14:50:36 [grok-build-0.1]     mov     isr, y
2026-09-04 14:50:36 [grok-build-0.1]     push
2026-09-04 14:50:36 [grok-build-0.1]     mov     isr, ~null     ; ← remove or comment this
