2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.269 677.1KB 16.42s
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] • Answer:*
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] In PIO, `push` always sends the current value of the ISR register. There is no
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] direct "push pins" instruction.
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] To push the pins value, you must first move the pins into the `ISR`, then `push`.
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] How to do it properly (without resetting the tick)
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] Here’s the correct pattern for the `changed` section if you want to push both tick and
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] pins while keeping the tick counter running continuously:
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] changed:
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1]     mov     y, x              ; y = new pins value
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1]     push                      ; 1. push tick (ISR currently holds the tick)
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1]     mov     isr, y            ; 2. put the new pins into ISR
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1]     push                      ; 3. push pins
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1]     ; 4. Restore the tick back into ISR so it can keep counting
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1]     mov     isr, x            ; (we saved the tick in x earlier)
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1]     jmp     loop
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] Full clean version of the `changed` block:
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] changed:
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1]     mov     y, x              ; y = new pins
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1]     mov     x, isr            ; save current tick into x
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1]     push                      ; push the tick
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1]     mov     isr, y            ; load pins into ISR
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1]     push                      ; push the pins
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1]     mov     isr, x            ; restore the tick so it continues decrementing
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1]     jmp     loop
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] Why this order?
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] - `push` can only push whatever is currently in `ISR`.
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] - We have to temporarily overwrite `ISR` with the pins to push them.
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] - We must put the tick value back into `ISR` afterwards, otherwise the normal decrement logic in
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] the `loop` will break.
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] Would you like a version where the tick counts up instead of down? That would be cleaner
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] for "not resetting".
2026-09-04 14:58:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
