2026-09-04 15:08:16 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 15:08:16 [grok-build-0.1] How "push pins" works here:
2026-09-04 15:08:16 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 15:08:16 [grok-build-0.1] `push` can only send whatever is currently in the `ISR` register.
2026-09-04 15:08:16 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 15:08:16 [grok-build-0.1] So the sequence is:
2026-09-04 15:08:16 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 15:08:16 [grok-build-0.1] 1. Save the tick (`isr`) into `x` so we don't lose it.
2026-09-04 15:08:16 [grok-build-0.1] 2. `push` → sends the tick.
2026-09-04 15:08:16 [grok-build-0.1] 3. Move the pins (`y`) into `ISR`.
2026-09-04 15:08:16 [grok-build-0.1] 4. `push` → now sends the pins.
2026-09-04 15:08:16 [grok-build-0.1] 5. Put the saved tick back into `ISR` so the normal decrement logic in the main loop can continue.
2026-09-04 15:08:16 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 15:08:16 [grok-build-0.1] This is the standard pattern when you need to push something other than the current `ISR` value
2026-09-04 15:08:16 [grok-build-0.1] without destroying your running counter.
2026-09-04 15:08:16 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-04 15:08:16 [grok-build-0.1] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
