2026-09-05 10:24:11 [grok-build-0.1] Yes, you need a root node.
2026-09-05 10:24:11 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 10:24:11 [grok-build-0.1] In Godot you cannot save a scene without at least one node (the root).
2026-09-05 10:24:11 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 10:24:11 [grok-build-0.1] Recommended way in the editor:
2026-09-05 10:24:11 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 10:24:11 [grok-build-0.1] 1. Go to Scene → New Scene.
2026-09-05 10:24:11 [grok-build-0.1] 2. Click Other Node.
2026-09-05 10:24:11 [grok-build-0.1] 3. Search for and select `Node` (this is the lightest root).
2026-09-05 10:24:11 [grok-build-0.1] 4. In the Scene dock, select the new `Node`.
2026-09-05 10:24:11 [grok-build-0.1] 5. Drag your `main.gd` file from the FileSystem onto the node (or click the Attach Script
2026-09-05 10:24:11 [grok-build-0.1] button in the toolbar).
2026-09-05 10:24:11 [grok-build-0.1] 6. Save the scene as `main.tscn` (File → Save Scene As...).
2026-09-05 10:24:11 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 10:24:11 [grok-build-0.1] After that:
2026-09-05 10:24:11 [grok-build-0.1] - Go to Project → Project Settings → Application → Run
2026-09-05 10:24:11 [grok-build-0.1] - Set Main Scene to `res://main.tscn`
2026-09-05 10:24:11 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 10:24:11 [grok-build-0.1] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
