2026-09-05 10:30:58 [grok-build-0.1] code.
2026-09-05 10:30:58 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 10:30:58 [grok-build-0.1] How it works:
2026-09-05 10:30:58 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 10:30:58 [grok-build-0.1] The `.tscn` file only stores the connection, like this:
2026-09-05 10:30:58 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 10:30:58 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 10:30:58 [grok-build-0.1] [ext_resource type="Script" path="res://control.gd" id="1"]
2026-09-05 10:30:58 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 10:30:58 [grok-build-0.1] [node name="Control" type="Control"]
2026-09-05 10:30:58 [grok-build-0.1] script = ExtResource("1")
2026-09-05 10:30:58 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 10:30:58 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 10:30:58 [grok-build-0.1] This tells Godot:
2026-09-05 10:30:58 [grok-build-0.1] 	Load the script from `control.gd` and attach it to this node."
2026-09-05 10:30:58 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 10:30:59 [grok-build-0.1] The actual code stays in the separate `control.gd` file. This separation is intentional — it keeps
2026-09-05 10:30:59 [grok-build-0.1] your scene file small and makes it easier to reuse scripts across different scenes.
2026-09-05 10:30:59 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 10:30:59 [grok-build-0.1] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-05 10:30:59 [grok-build-0.1] 
