2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.022 59.61KB 9.38s
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] • No.*
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] Standard JSON-RPC 2.0 does not have any built-in mechanism for ACKs with checksums.
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] What JSON-RPC actually gives you
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] ╭──────────────────────────────┬────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] │  Feature                     │  JSON-RPC 2.0  │  Notes                                            │
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] ├──────────────────────────────┼────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] │  Request / Response          │  Yes           │  Uses `id` to match requests with replies         │
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] │  Error handling              │  Yes           │  `"error"` field in response                      │
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] │  Notifications (no reply)    │  Yes           │  Send without `id`                                │
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] │  Checksum / Integrity        │  **No**        │  Not part of the spec                             │
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] │  Automatic ACK               │  **No**        │  Only the response itself                         │
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] │  Duplicate detection         │  **No**        │  You have to implement it                         │
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] │  Ordering / Reliability      │  **No**        │  WebSocket doesn't guarantee order on reconnects  │
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] ╰──────────────────────────────┴────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] Current situation in your project
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] You're currently using JSON-RPC requests for everything (including `tick`), and Godot is
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] replying with the full original payload. This is why you're getting huge nested echoes.
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] For something like a `tick` (which looks like a heartbeat or counter), you usually have two
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] choices:
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] Options
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] • Option 1: Use JSON-RPC properly (recommended for now)*
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] - Only send a response when you actually need to acknowledge something.
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] - For `tick`, you can treat it as a notification (no reply needed), or send a very
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] lightweight reply.
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] • Option 2: Add your own checksum layer on top*
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] If you want integrity checking on the payload, you have to add it yourself. Example:
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1] {
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1]   "jsonrpc": "2.0",
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1]   "id": 42,
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1]   "method": "tick",
2026-09-05 10:57:30 [grok-build-0.1]   "params": {
