2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.041 99.14KB 9.03s
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] Yes, a scene can run on a texture in Godot.
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] This is a very common technique. Here's what you should understand about it:
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] What "running on a texture" actually means
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] In Godot, you can render an entire scene (or part of one) into a texture instead of directly to the
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] screen. This texture can then be used in multiple ways:
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] - Displayed inside a 2D UI (e.g. inside your current `Control` scene)
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] - Applied as a material on a 3D object
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] - Used for effects like portals, mirrors, minimaps, or security cameras
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] The main tool: SubViewport
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] The standard way to do this in Godot 4 is with a `SubViewport`.
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] Key ideas:
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] - A `SubViewport` acts like a separate render target.
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] - You can put a full scene (including 3D nodes and GLB instances) inside it.
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] - You can then grab the result as a texture and display it wherever you want.
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] - The scene inside the SubViewport runs normally (its scripts, physics, etc. still work).
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] How this relates to your current project
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] You're currently using a `Control` as the root. This makes direct 3D rendering awkward. Rendering a
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] scene onto a texture is actually one of the cleanest ways to solve this without changing your root
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] node structure.
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] Common patterns:
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] - Keep your 2D UI and log view on the main scene.
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] - Put your 3D GLB spawning logic inside a `SubViewport`.
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] - Show the 3D content in your UI using a `TextureRect` or `SubViewportContainer`.
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] Important things to consider
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] 1. Performance
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1]    Rendering a scene to a texture has a cost. If you're spawning lots of GLBs or running complex
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] scenes inside the viewport, it can get expensive.
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] 2. Input
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1]    If the scene inside the texture needs to receive mouse/keyboard input, you have to handle
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] forwarding that input yourself. It's not automatic.
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] 3. Size & Resolution
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1]    The SubViewport has its own size. If you make it too small, the output will look pixelated. If
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] too big, it wastes performance.
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] 4. Ownership
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1]    The scene running inside the SubViewport is still a normal scene. It can have its own root node,
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] scripts, and even its own child nodes being spawned.
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] 5. Alternatives
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1]    - Change your main scene root to `Node3D` + overlay 2D on top (more "traditional" 3D project
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] structure).
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1]    - Use multiple scenes and switch between them.
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1]    - Render to texture only for specific effects.
2026-09-05 11:29:35 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] Questions worth thinking about
2026-09-05 11:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] - Do you want the 3D content to appear inside your current 2D interface (like a window or
2026-09-05 11:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] panel), or do you eventually want the whole project to be 3D with UI on top?
2026-09-05 11:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] - Will the spawned GLB scenes need to be interactive, or are they mostly visual?
2026-09-05 11:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] - Are you thinking of having multiple separate "scenes" running at the same time on different
2026-09-05 11:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] textures?
2026-09-05 11:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] Would you like to discuss the trade-offs between using a SubViewport versus restructuring the
2026-09-05 11:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] project root?
2026-09-05 11:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] 
