2026-09-05 11:36:05 [grok-build-0.1] Filtering is often the enemy of crisp vector lines.
2026-09-05 11:36:05 [grok-build-0.1] - Whether the viewport itself uses any MSAA.
2026-09-05 11:36:05 [grok-build-0.1] - Pixel snapping behavior in the 2D canvas.
2026-09-05 11:36:05 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:36:05 [grok-build-0.1] For clean lines, you usually want the output texture to be sampled without linear filtering
2026-09-05 11:36:05 [grok-build-0.1] when possible.
2026-09-05 11:36:05 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:36:05 [grok-build-0.1] • 4. Coordinate Mapping and Precision*
2026-09-05 11:36:05 [grok-build-0.1] Since this is tied to a 6502 simulator:
2026-09-05 11:36:05 [grok-build-0.1] - How you map the simulator’s vector coordinates into the SubViewport space matters.
2026-09-05 11:36:05 [grok-build-0.1] - Floating point precision and snapping can destroy line quality.
2026-09-05 11:36:05 [grok-build-0.1] - You want a predictable, stable mapping that doesn’t introduce jitter or uneven line thickness.
2026-09-05 11:36:05 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:36:05 [grok-build-0.1] • 5. Batching and Redraw Strategy*
2026-09-05 11:36:05 [grok-build-0.1] For a baseline, keep the drawing model simple:
2026-09-05 11:36:05 [grok-build-0.1] - Are you clearing and redrawing all vectors every frame?
