2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] • Good question.* Let's look at this realistically for your use case.
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] How clean can glTF represent a 2D vector line list?
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] • Short answer:*
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] It can be reasonably clean if you treat it purely as a data container (using its buffer
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] system), but it is not a natural fit for per-frame, discardable 2D vector data.
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] Best Way to Represent It in glTF
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] The cleanest representation in glTF would be:
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] - Use a Mesh with `mode: 1` (LINES)
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] - Use `POSITION` accessor with `componentType: 5126` (FLOAT) and `type: "VEC2"`
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] - Store the data in a buffer as a flat list of `x, y` pairs
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] - Each pair of vertices = one line segment
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] Example logical structure:
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] Buffer (raw bytes)
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] [
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1]   x1, y1,     // start of line 1
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1]   x2, y2,     // end of line 1
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1]   x3, y3,     // start of line 2
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1]   x4, y4,     // end of line 2
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1]   ...
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] ]
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] This maps directly to what you asked for ("use buffer for x,y").
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] You can also add:
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] - A second accessor (`COLOR_0`) for brightness per vertex (or per line if you duplicate vertices).
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] - This keeps brightness data alongside the coordinates in the buffer system.
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] Cleanliness Assessment
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] ╭─────────────────────────────┬────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] │  Aspect                     │  How Clean?        │  Notes                                                        │
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] ├─────────────────────────────┼────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] │  Buffer layout (x,y)        │  Quite clean       │  Simple flat VEC2 works well                                  │
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] │  Line representation        │  Moderately clean  │  `mode: 1` (LINES) is the proper way                          │
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] │  Brightness / intensity     │  Acceptable        │  Can use COLOR_0, but feels bolted on                         │
2026-09-05 11:41:33 [grok-build-0.1] │  Per-frame updates          │  **Poor**          │  glTF is not designed for this                                │
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] │  Overhead                   │  High              │  Lots of JSON metadata for what is basically a list of lines  │
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] │  Loading in Godot           │  Awkward           │  Standard importer is heavy for this                          │
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] │  Simplicity                 │  Low               │  More complex than needed                                     │
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] ╰─────────────────────────────┴────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] Important Limitations for Your Case
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] - glTF is overkill here. You're sending simple, discardable line lists every frame.
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] Wrapping them in a full glTF asset (with accessors, bufferViews, meshes, etc.) adds significant
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] parsing and structural overhead for very little benefit.
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] - Not great for streaming. Godot’s glTF loader is built for loading models once, not for
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] receiving and discarding hundreds of tiny “models” per second.
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] - Coordinate system friction. glTF expects 3D data by default. Forcing it into 2D requires
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] extra care (especially with Y-axis direction).
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] - You’d likely end up parsing the buffers manually anyway rather than using Godot’s normal
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] glTF import pipeline.
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] When This Approach Makes Sense
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] Using glTF buffers would only really be worth it if you had one or more of these goals:
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] - You want to reuse existing glTF tooling / exporters on the emulator side.
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] - You plan to eventually attach more data (UVs, normals, etc.) later.
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] - You want a standardized binary + JSON container format.
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] For a pure vector display peripheral with simple redraws, this is usually more complexity than
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] necessary.
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] Better Alternatives (for context)
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] Since you want to keep it simple:
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] - Raw JSON array of lines (`[[x1,y1,x2,y2], ...]`)
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] - A very small custom binary format (just a header + float buffer)
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] - MessagePack or similar (compact but still structured)
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] These are all significantly cleaner than glTF for this specific workload.
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] • Quick question to narrow it down:*
2026-09-05 11:41:34 [grok-build-0.1] 
