2026-09-05 11:43:13 [grok-build-0.1] Important Practical Considerations
2026-09-05 11:43:13 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:43:13 [grok-build-0.1] 1. Don't use Godot's normal glTF importer per frame
2026-09-05 11:43:13 [grok-build-0.1]    - The built-in `load("res://...")` or `GLTFDocument` import path is far too slow and heavy for
2026-09-05 11:43:13 [grok-build-0.1] per-frame data.
2026-09-05 11:43:13 [grok-build-0.1]    - You would need to manually parse the glTF (or .glb) and extract the buffers yourself
2026-09-05 11:43:13 [grok-build-0.1] using `GLTFDocument` + `GLTFState` in a lightweight way, or parse the binary directly.
2026-09-05 11:43:13 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:43:13 [grok-build-0.1] 2. JSON vs Binary (.glb)
2026-09-05 11:43:13 [grok-build-0.1]    - `.gltf` (JSON + separate buffer) = very chatty for small line lists.
2026-09-05 11:43:13 [grok-build-0.1]    - `.glb` (single binary) = much better, but still carries glTF metadata overhead (scenes, nodes,
2026-09-05 11:43:13 [grok-build-0.1] accessors, bufferViews, etc.).
2026-09-05 11:43:13 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:43:13 [grok-build-0.1] 3. Coordinate System Friction
2026-09-05 11:43:13 [grok-build-0.1]    - glTF uses right-handed Y-up by default. For a 2D vector display you'll need to decide on a
2026-09-05 11:43:13 [grok-build-0.1] consistent mapping (and document it).
2026-09-05 11:43:13 [grok-build-0.1]    - Using `VEC2` in `POSITION` is allowed, but many tools and parts of the spec assume 3D.
2026-09-05 11:43:13 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:43:13 [grok-build-0.1] 4. Future-Proofing vs Current Overhead
2026-09-05 11:43:13 [grok-build-0.1]    Since glTF is first-class in your design, the real question becomes:
2026-09-05 11:43:13 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:43:13 [grok-build-0.1]    - Are you willing to pay the parsing + structure cost now so that vector data, 3D models, and
2026-09-05 11:43:13 [grok-build-0.1] future assets all live in the same format?
2026-09-05 11:43:14 [grok-build-0.1]    - Or do you want a lighter custom format for the vector stream and only use glTF for
2026-09-05 11:43:14 [grok-build-0.1] static/heavier assets?
2026-09-05 11:43:14 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:43:14 [grok-build-0.1] Realistic Recommendation
2026-09-05 11:43:14 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:43:14 [grok-build-0.1] If glTF must be first-class, then the cleanest way forward is:
2026-09-05 11:43:14 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:43:14 [grok-build-0.1] - Send minimal .glb files (or raw glTF JSON + buffer) containing only a single mesh in
2026-09-05 11:43:14 [grok-build-0.1] `LINES` mode.
2026-09-05 11:43:14 [grok-build-0.1] - On the Godot side, write a small dedicated parser that extracts the `POSITION` and `COLOR_0`
2026-09-05 11:43:14 [grok-build-0.1] buffers directly, bypassing the full scene import.
2026-09-05 11:43:14 [grok-build-0.1] - Accept that there will be some structural overhead compared to a raw line list.
2026-09-05 11:43:14 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:43:14 [grok-build-0.1] This is doable, but it won't be as "simple" as sending a raw array of lines.
2026-09-05 11:43:14 [grok-build-0.1] 
