2026-09-05 11:44:13 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:44:13 [grok-build-0.1] You plan to receive data over the wire (WebSocket) and manually interpret it in GDScript,
2026-09-05 11:44:13 [grok-build-0.1] rather than using Godot’s built-in glTF loader.
2026-09-05 11:44:13 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:44:13 [grok-build-0.1] Given that glTF is meant to be a first-class citizen in the design, here’s what actually matters in
2026-09-05 11:44:13 [grok-build-0.1] this scenario:
2026-09-05 11:44:13 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:44:13 [grok-build-0.1] 1. Parsing Cost in GDScript
2026-09-05 11:44:13 [grok-build-0.1] This is the biggest practical constraint.
2026-09-05 11:44:13 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:44:13 [grok-build-0.1] Manually parsing glTF in GDScript every frame has real overhead:
2026-09-05 11:44:13 [grok-build-0.1] - JSON parsing (`JSON.parse_string`) is relatively expensive.
2026-09-05 11:44:13 [grok-build-0.1] - Walking the glTF structure (accessors, bufferViews, buffers) adds more work.
2026-09-05 11:44:13 [grok-build-0.1] - Extracting binary data (especially if base64 encoded) is even heavier.
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] For a high-frequency vector display (even if lines are discardable), this can become noticeably
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] slow if the frame rate of the DVG is high or the number of lines is large.
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] 2. How Much of glTF Do You Actually Need?
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] Since you're interpreting it yourself, you don’t have to support the full spec. This is where you
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] can keep things reasonable.
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] The minimal useful subset for your case would likely be:
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] - One mesh
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] - One primitive with `mode: 1` (LINES)
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] - `POSITION` accessor (VEC2)
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] - Optionally `COLOR_0` for brightness
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] - The buffer data itself
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] Everything else (scenes, nodes, materials, animations, etc.) can be ignored.
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] The question becomes: how much of the glTF structure are you willing to carry just because glTF is
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] “first class”?
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] 3. Binary vs Text Delivery
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] This affects cleanliness significantly:
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] - JSON glTF + base64 buffer → Easy to send in JSON-RPC, but very wasteful and slow to
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] decode in GDScript.
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] - .glb (binary) → Much more efficient, but now you have to parse a binary format manually
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] in GDScript (header + chunks). This is doable but more error-prone.
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] - Hybrid → Send a very small glTF JSON that references a separate binary blob you send
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] alongside it.
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] For per-frame vector data, pure JSON + base64 is usually the worst choice for performance.
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] 4. Trade-off Between Standardization and Simplicity
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] This is the core tension right now:
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] - Pro-glTF: You get a standardized, well-defined buffer layout for coordinates and
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] attributes. Other tools can potentially generate or consume the same data. It stays consistent with
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] how you handle real 3D models elsewhere.
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] - Con-glTF: You pay a structural and parsing tax for something that is fundamentally just a
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] list of 2D lines. In GDScript, a raw array or a tiny custom binary format would be dramatically
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] simpler and faster to interpret.
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] Because you’re doing the interpretation yourself, the “first class” benefit mostly comes from the
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] data model, not from using Godot’s loader.
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] 5. Other Practical Considerations
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] - Brightness: Easy to carry in `COLOR_0`, but you’ll have to decide whether to send it
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] per-vertex or per-line (duplicating vertices).
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] - Coordinate system: You’ll need to define and enforce a consistent mapping (especially Y
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] direction) since glTF is 3D-oriented by default.
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] - Validation: If you only support a tiny subset, you should be strict about what you
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] accept. Loose parsing can hide problems.
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] - Future evolution: If glTF is first-class, you may later want to send more complex vector
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] data (curves, multiple layers, etc.). Designing the minimal structure now with that in mind helps.
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] Realistic Assessment for Your Baseline
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] If you go this route, the cleanest outcome is probably:
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] - Accept a very restricted form of glTF (or .glb).
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] - Write a small dedicated parser that only looks for the line list buffers.
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] - Treat anything outside that subset as invalid.
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:44:14 [grok-build-0.1] Even then, it will be more code and slower than sending a simple list of lines.
