2026-09-05 11:45:04 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:45:04 [grok-build-0.1] This keeps the structure very flat. You’re basically just using glTF’s accessor + buffer system to
2026-09-05 11:45:04 [grok-build-0.1] describe a list of line segments.
2026-09-05 11:45:04 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:45:04 [grok-build-0.1] Key Design Points
2026-09-05 11:45:04 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:45:04 [grok-build-0.1] ╭─────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-09-05 11:45:04 [grok-build-0.1] │  Aspect                     │  Recommendation                                       │  Why it matters for you                            │
2026-09-05 11:45:04 [grok-build-0.1] ├─────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-09-05 11:45:04 [grok-build-0.1] │  **POSITION**               │  Use `VEC2` (not VEC3)                                │  Keeps data minimal and honest for 2D              │
2026-09-05 11:45:04 [grok-build-0.1] │  **Buffer layout**          │  Flat interleaved or separate accessors               │  Simple to walk in GDScript                        │
2026-09-05 11:45:04 [grok-build-0.1] │  **Brightness**             │  `COLOR_0` (as vec4 or just alpha)                    │  Natural place to put intensity                    │
2026-09-05 11:45:04 [grok-build-0.1] │  **Metadata**               │  Keep it extremely small                              │  You don’t want to parse scenes/nodes every frame  │
2026-09-05 11:45:04 [grok-build-0.1] │  **Per-frame nature**       │  Accept that each message is essentially one mesh     │  No need for full scene graphs                     │
2026-09-05 11:45:04 [grok-build-0.1] ╰─────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-09-05 11:45:04 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:45:04 [grok-build-0.1] Things Worth Deciding Early
2026-09-05 11:45:04 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:45:05 [grok-build-0.1] 1. How much glTF structure do you actually want to carry?
2026-09-05 11:45:05 [grok-build-0.1]    - Do you want a full valid glTF (with `asset`, `scenes`, `nodes`, etc.) even if you ignore most
2026-09-05 11:45:05 [grok-build-0.1] of it?
2026-09-05 11:45:05 [grok-build-0.1]    - Or are you okay with a very stripped-down glTF that only contains the mesh + buffers?
2026-09-05 11:45:05 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:45:05 [grok-build-0.1] 2. Brightness handling
2026-09-05 11:45:05 [grok-build-0.1]    - Do you want brightness per line (you’d need to duplicate vertices), or per vertex?
2026-09-05 11:45:05 [grok-build-0.1]    - Are you happy using `COLOR_0`, or would you rather put intensity in a custom attribute?
2026-09-05 11:45:05 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:45:05 [grok-build-0.1] 3. Coordinate system
2026-09-05 11:45:05 [grok-build-0.1]    - Will you normalize on the sending side (e.g. -1 to 1), or send raw DVG-style integer
2026-09-05 11:45:05 [grok-build-0.1] coordinates and scale in Godot?
2026-09-05 11:45:05 [grok-build-0.1]    - Y-axis direction needs to be consistent.
2026-09-05 11:45:05 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:45:05 [grok-build-0.1] 4. Format on the wire
2026-09-05 11:45:05 [grok-build-0.1]    - Since JSON parsing is acceptable, are you leaning toward `.gltf` (JSON + base64 buffers) or
2026-09-05 11:45:05 [grok-build-0.1] `.glb` (binary)?
2026-09-05 11:45:05 [grok-build-0.1]    - `.glb` is cleaner as data, but harder to construct manually on the simulator side.
2026-09-05 11:45:05 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:45:05 [grok-build-0.1] Realistic Cleanliness
2026-09-05 11:45:05 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:45:05 [grok-build-0.1] If glTF is genuinely first-class in the design, then using a minimal mesh + VEC2 buffer is probably
2026-09-05 11:45:05 [grok-build-0.1] the least ugly way to do it. It’s not the absolute simplest possible format, but it gives you a
2026-09-05 11:45:05 [grok-build-0.1] consistent, documented way to describe line lists that could also be generated or inspected by
2026-09-05 11:45:05 [grok-build-0.1] other tools.
2026-09-05 11:45:05 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:45:05 [grok-build-0.1] However, because you’re manually interpreting it, you will still be doing a lot of the work
2026-09-05 11:45:05 [grok-build-0.1] yourself (walking accessors, resolving bufferViews, etc.). The "first class" benefit mostly comes
2026-09-05 11:45:05 [grok-build-0.1] from the data model and future compatibility rather than convenience in GDScript.
2026-09-05 11:45:05 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:45:05 [grok-build-0.1] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-05 11:45:05 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:45:05 [grok-build-0.1] Would you like to go deeper on the actual buffer + accessor layout you should aim for, or would you
2026-09-05 11:45:05 [grok-build-0.1] rather discuss how much of the glTF wrapper you’re willing to keep versus strip down?
