2026-09-05 11:46:02 [nitro@ryzen5] so passing json and buffers over rpc, any thoughts
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.054 151.2KB 13.99s
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] • Yes, let's talk about it directly.*
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] You're planning to send glTF-structured data (JSON + binary buffers) over your JSON-RPC
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] style WebSocket connection for per-frame vector lines. Here are the real considerations:
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] 1. How You Actually Transport "JSON + Buffers"
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] You have a few practical options over WebSocket:
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] ╭─────────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] │  Approach                       │  Format                                  │  Pros                                       │  Cons                                                       │  Recommendation                               │
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] ├─────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] │  **Full glTF as JSON**          │  JSON with base64 buffers inside         │  Easy to debug, fits naturally in JSON-RPC  │  ~33% size bloat from base64, verbose                       │  Only okay for small frames                   │
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] │  **.glb as base64**             │  JSON-RPC with one big base64 string     │  Still text-based, one field                │  Same base64 overhead, ugly for large data                  │  Acceptable if frames stay small              │
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] │  **Hybrid (recommended path)**  │  JSON metadata + separate binary buffer  │  Efficient, clean separation                │  More complex on both sides (especially if using JSON-RPC)  │  Usually best                                 │
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] │  **Raw .glb binary**            │  Send as binary WebSocket frame          │  Most efficient                             │  Breaks "pure JSON-RPC" model                               │  Good if you're willing to mix text + binary  │
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] ╰─────────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] Since you're currently using a JSON-RPC style, the cleanest compromise is usually sending the glTF
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] JSON with base64-encoded buffers inside it, at least to start.
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] 2. Important Practical Issues
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] - Overhead
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1]   Even a minimal glTF has quite a bit of structural JSON (`accessors`, `bufferViews`, `buffers`,
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] `meshes`, etc.). For a vector frame with only a few hundred lines, this can easily be larger than
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] the actual line data.
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] - Base64 penalty
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1]   If you embed the coordinate buffer as base64, you pay ~33% extra size every frame. For a vector
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] display this can add up quickly.
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] - Parsing in GDScript
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1]   You will need to:
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1]   1. Parse the outer JSON-RPC message
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1]   2. Parse the inner glTF JSON
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1]   3. Decode base64 (if used)
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1]   4. Walk the accessors to extract the `POSITION` buffer
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1]   This is doable, but it's more steps than just receiving a flat array of lines.
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] - Message framing
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1]   How will you distinguish between:
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1]   - A normal RPC call (`tick`, `init`, etc.)
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1]   - A vector frame (which is really just data, not a command)?
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1]   You'll probably want a dedicated method like `"vectorFrame"` or `"displayFrame"` rather than
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] trying to reuse `tick`.
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] 3. Design Thoughts Specific to Your Setup
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1] - Keep the glTF extremely minimal.
2026-09-05 11:46:17 [grok-build-0.1]   Don't send full scenes, nodes, or materials every frame. Ideally you only send the mesh + one
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1] primitive + the buffer data. Anything else is noise.
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1] - Consider separating concerns
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1]   You could send a small JSON-RPC envelope that contains either:
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1]   - A full minimal glTF (as string or object), or
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1]   - Just the raw buffer data + a tiny schema description.
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1]   Many people in this situation eventually move toward a custom lightweight format that
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1] looks like glTF's buffer style but without the full wrapper.
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1] - Versioning
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1]   If glTF is first-class, you should think about how you'll evolve the format later (adding more
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1] attributes, different primitive types, etc.). Putting a small version field at the top level is
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1] worth doing early.
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1] - Binary vs Text WebSocket
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1]   WebSockets can send binary frames. If you ever want to drop base64, you could send the actual
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1] `.glb` bytes as a binary message instead of wrapping everything in JSON. This is worth keeping in
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1] mind even if you start with JSON.
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1] 4. Realistic Assessment
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1] For a simple baseline, sending glTF (even minimal) per frame over JSON-RPC is workable, but
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1] it is not the lightest path. The main value you get is consistency with your "glTF is first-class"
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1] goal and a well-defined way to describe the line data.
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1] The friction points will be:
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1] - Structural bloat
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1] - Base64 cost (if you use it)
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1] - Having to manually walk the glTF structure every frame in GDScript
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1] Would you like to discuss a concrete message shape? For example:
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1] - What a minimal per-frame glTF message might look like inside your JSON-RPC
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1] - Whether to embed the buffer as base64 or move toward binary frames
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1] - How to structure the RPC method itself (`vectorFrame`, etc.)
2026-09-05 11:46:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
