2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] • Yes*, `PackedVector2Array` is a native Godot type.
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] What it actually is:
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] - It is a built-in specialized array in Godot (not a regular `Array`).
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] - It is specifically optimized to store `Vector2` values.
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] - It lives in the engine's core, written in C++ for performance.
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] Key characteristics:
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] - It is typed — it can only hold `Vector2` values.
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] - It is more memory-efficient and faster than a normal `Array` of `Vector2`s.
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] - It has its own methods (like `append()`, `size()`, indexing, etc.).
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] - It is commonly used when dealing with geometry, drawing, mesh data, or anything
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] performance-sensitive involving 2D positions.
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] Comparison:
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] ╭───────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────┬───────────────┬────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] │  Type                     │  Can hold only Vector2?  │  Performance  │  Memory usage  │  Typical use case                      │
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] ├───────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────┼───────────────┼────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] │  `Array`                  │  No                      │  Lower        │  Higher        │  General purpose                       │
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] │  `PackedVector2Array`     │  Yes                     │  Higher       │  Lower         │  Lines, paths, geometry, drawing       │
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] │  `PackedFloat32Array`     │  No                      │  High         │  Low           │  Raw buffers, audio, etc.              │
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] ╰───────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────┴───────────────┴────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] Relevance to your vector display:
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] Since you're planning to draw lines every frame from data coming over the wire,
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] `PackedVector2Array` is a natural fit. It’s the kind of structure Godot expects when doing things
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] like:
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] - Passing data into drawing functions
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] - Working with `RenderingServer` (if you go lower level later)
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] - Storing geometry data efficiently
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] It’s generally cleaner and faster than using a regular `Array` when you're dealing with large
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] numbers of 2D points.
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-05 11:50:23 [grok-build-0.1] 
