2026-09-05 13:51:18 [nitro@ryzen5] what is a meta float argument?
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.001 1.440KB 32.98s
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] In the context of Godot, a "meta float argument" usually refers to something
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] specific in Godot’s internal API description (like in `extension_api.json` or when using
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] GDExtension).
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] What it actually means:
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] When Godot describes a method or function (for example, for C++ bindings, GDExtension, or editor
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] introspection), each argument has this structure:
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] {
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1]   "name": "duration",
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1]   "type": "float",
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1]   "meta": "float"
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] }
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] - `"type": "float"` → tells you it’s a floating-point number.
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] - `"meta": "float"` → gives more precise information about the float type.
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] Why does the "meta" field exist?
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] Godot supports two floating-point precisions:
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] ╭────────────────────────┬──────────────────┬───────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] │  Build Type            │  `"meta"` value  │  Actual C++ type      │  Notes                                   │
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] ├────────────────────────┼──────────────────┼───────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] │  **Single** precision  │  `"float"`       │  `float`              │  Default in most builds                  │
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] │  **Double** precision  │  `"double"`      │  `double`             │  Used in scientific/precise simulations  │
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] ╰────────────────────────┴──────────────────┴───────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] So when you see `"meta": "float"`, it means the argument expects a normal single-precision float.
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] Common places you’ll see this:
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] - `extension_api.json` (the file Godot generates for GDExtension)
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] - When inspecting methods via `ClassDB`
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] - When creating GDExtension bindings in C++
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] - In some generated binding code or JSON project data
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] Example
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] A method signature might be described like this:
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1] {
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1]   "name": "set_speed",
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1]   "arguments": [
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1]     {
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1]       "name": "value",
2026-09-05 13:51:52 [grok-build-0.1]       "type": "float",
2026-09-05 13:51:53 [grok-build-0.1]       "meta": "float"
2026-09-05 13:51:53 [grok-build-0.1]     }
2026-09-05 13:51:53 [grok-build-0.1]   ]
2026-09-05 13:51:53 [grok-build-0.1] }
2026-09-05 13:51:53 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 13:51:53 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 13:51:53 [grok-build-0.1] This means the function takes a float argument.
2026-09-05 13:51:53 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 13:51:53 [grok-build-0.1] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-05 13:51:53 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 13:51:53 [grok-build-0.1] • Would you like me to explain how this differs between single and double precision builds, or
2026-09-05 13:51:53 [grok-build-0.1] how to handle float arguments when writing GDExtension code?*
