2026-09-05 13:53:09 [grok-build-0.1] ├───────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────┤
2026-09-05 13:53:09 [grok-build-0.1] │  Serious 2D game                      │  Yes                     │  Good choice             │
2026-09-05 13:53:09 [grok-build-0.1] │  Mid-sized 3D game                    │  Yes, with caveats       │  Viable                  │
2026-09-05 13:53:09 [grok-build-0.1] │  Large / high-budget 3D game          │  Risky                   │  Consider Unreal         │
2026-09-05 13:53:09 [grok-build-0.1] │  Multiplayer game (complex)           │  Possible                │  Requires extra work     │
2026-09-05 13:53:09 [grok-build-0.1] │  Solo or small team serious game      │  Yes                     │  Very reasonable choice  │
2026-09-05 13:53:09 [grok-build-0.1] ╰───────────────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────╯
2026-09-05 13:53:09 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 13:53:09 [grok-build-0.1] Bottom Line
2026-09-05 13:53:09 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-05 13:53:09 [grok-build-0.1] Godot is mature enough for many serious commercial games, especially 2D or medium-scope 3D
2026-09-05 13:53:09 [grok-build-0.1] titles. It is no longer just a "hobby engine." However, it still requires more technical effort in
2026-09-05 13:53:09 [grok-build-0.1] some areas compared to Unity or Unreal if you're pushing for high production values or very large
2026-09-05 13:53:09 [grok-build-0.1] scope.
