2026-09-06 19:15:54 [deepseek-v4-flash] It doesn’t fry valid UTF‑8 — it just copies every byte through. If the input `std::string` contains
2026-09-06 19:15:54 [deepseek-v4-flash] UTF‑8 text, the bytes are already multi‑byte sequences; the loop sees each byte in turn and pushes
2026-09-06 19:15:54 [deepseek-v4-flash] it into `out`. The sequence is preserved exactly.
2026-09-06 19:15:54 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-06 19:15:54 [deepseek-v4-flash] For example, the input `"héllo"` is the bytes `68 C3 A9 6C 6C 6F`. The function will output `"h`
2026-09-06 19:15:54 [deepseek-v4-flash] then `C3` then `A9` then `llo` — the same bytes inside quotes. That’s still the same UTF‑8 string.
2026-09-06 19:15:54 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-06 19:15:54 [deepseek-v4-flash] JSON itself allows raw Unicode characters in strings when the document is encoded as UTF‑8. So this
2026-09-06 19:15:54 [deepseek-v4-flash] output is valid.
2026-09-06 19:15:54 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-06 19:15:54 [deepseek-v4-flash] The only case it would produce invalid JSON is if the input contains invalid UTF‑8 byte sequences.
2026-09-06 19:15:54 [deepseek-v4-flash] If your source strings are always valid UTF‑8, you’re fine.
2026-09-06 19:15:54 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-06 19:15:54 [deepseek-v4-flash] If you meant you wanted non‑ASCII escaped as `\uXXXX` so the output is pure ASCII, that’s a
2026-09-06 19:15:54 [deepseek-v4-flash] different requirement — and that’s what the earlier more complex code could do. But you said you
2026-09-06 19:15:54 [deepseek-v4-flash] were happy leaving Unicode unescaped, so the simple version does exactly that.
2026-09-06 19:15:54 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.071 53.90KB 402.88s
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] I read this as a bus-capture/trace dump, so the first thing I’d do is filter before trying to
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] decode instructions.
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] Timestamp deltas
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] The PIO timestamp is a countdown in `isr`, and it is only decremented on the no-change
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] path. On the `changed` path the current tick is pushed, but not advanced before the next compare.
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] So consecutive rows with the same `t` are not actually “zero time apart.” They mean the pin field
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] changed on consecutive PIO sample opportunities without a stable sample in between. That is exactly
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] the kind of settling/jitter burst you are seeing.
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] For a first filter in C++:
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Group samples by identical, wrap-aware timestamp.
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Keep only the last sample in each group.
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] - The last sample is the one that appears to have settled, because the next sample with a different
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] timestamp means there was finally a quiet/no-change interval.
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] Also, `pinStamp` is only printing 4 hex digits of a 32-bit tick. That will make the countdown look
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] non-monotonic when the low 16 bits wrap. The first few timestamps in the trace already look suspect
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] (`9d36`, `adb2`, `219a`, `09ba`, then `c740`). For delta work, keep the full 32-bit tick and use
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] modulo subtraction.
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] PIO-side jitter detection/filtering
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] PIO can detect repeated transitions, but the current PIO program records them all. A PIO debounce
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] is possible in principle:
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] - On the first change, do not immediately push.
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Wait one more sample period and compare again.
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Only push if the same value is still present.
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] That will reject very short single-sample bounces, but it costs extra PIO state and makes the
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] timestamp semantics trickier. The easier path is probably to leave PIO as a fast edge-catcher and
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] filter bursts in C++/host code using the timestamp grouping above.
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] One important PIO detail: the state machine appears to be running continuously before DMA starts,
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] so the RX FIFO may already contain old events. Clear the FIFO, or restart/reset the PWM, before
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] starting each capture. Otherwise the first samples in the trace can be stale.
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] Watch out for undecoded GPIO bits
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] PIO `pins` samples all 32 GPIOs, including GPIO25 if the green LED is being blinked. The decoded
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] output in `pinStamp` does not show GPIO25, so an LED transition can produce a sample that looks
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] like an exact duplicate of the previous bus state.
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] If the LED/blink function is active during capture, mask those unused bits out of the
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] change-detection logic or stop blinking while capturing. Otherwise you will generate spurious
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] samples and odd timestamp groups.
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 6502 opcode/operand identification
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] There is no reliable “single opcode bit” that tells you whether a fetched byte is an opcode or
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] operand, or how many following bytes belong to the instruction. The 6502 opcode map does not encode
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] instruction length in one simple bit.
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] The reliable way to get instruction boundaries is to use the 6502 `SYNC` output. SYNC is high on
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] the opcode-fetch cycle. If you can bring SYNC into a spare GPIO and include it in the captured pin
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] word, then:
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] - A `sync=1` sample marks a new instruction fetch.
2026-09-07 07:47:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] - The data byte on that sample is the opcode.
2026-09-07 07:47:15 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Follow it with the operand bytes based on a proper 6502 opcode-length table.
2026-09-07 07:47:15 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 07:47:15 [deepseek-v4-flash] Without SYNC, you can sometimes start from a known code boundary and decode forward, but any
2026-09-07 07:47:15 [deepseek-v4-flash] interrupt or unknown boundary will desynchronise the stream. The bus trace alone cannot reliably
2026-09-07 07:47:15 [deepseek-v4-flash] distinguish an opcode fetch from an operand/data byte fetch.
2026-09-07 07:47:15 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 07:47:15 [deepseek-v4-flash] Build/code issues
2026-09-07 07:47:15 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 07:47:15 [deepseek-v4-flash] Two obvious issues in the shared source:
2026-09-07 07:47:15 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 07:47:15 [deepseek-v4-flash] - `snoop.cpp` creates a config variable, but then the `sm_config_set_*` calls and `pio_sm_init()`
2026-09-07 07:47:15 [deepseek-v4-flash] use `&c`. That will not compile unless `c` is declared or renamed to the config variable.
2026-09-07 07:47:15 [deepseek-v4-flash] - `stringify()` in `romulon.cpp` uses `text` but does not take a `text` argument, while `main()`
2026-09-07 07:47:15 [deepseek-v4-flash] calls `stringify(picoTitle)`. The signature needs to accept the string parameter.
2026-09-07 07:47:15 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 07:47:15 [deepseek-v4-flash] Once the trace is filtered to settled bus states, and SYNC/opcode-length table is available, this
2026-09-07 07:47:15 [deepseek-v4-flash] should become much more readable as a real 6502 instruction trace.
2026-09-07 07:47:15 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
