2026-09-07 08:08:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] The short answer is:
2026-09-07 08:08:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:08:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] • Don’t try to deduce SYNC from the captured address/data trace. Wire SYNC in instead.*
2026-09-07 08:08:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] There is no reliable bus-only signature that says “this byte is an opcode, not an operand or data
2026-09-07 08:08:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] byte.”
2026-09-07 08:08:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:08:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] The good news: you don’t need another spare GPIO. You already have one that is logically redundant
2026-09-07 08:08:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] — the ROM `CS` line.
2026-09-07 08:08:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:08:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] Why `CS` is redundant on the 1541
2026-09-07 08:08:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:08:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] The `CS` pin on the 2364 ROM socket is not an independent CPU signal. It is just the 1541’s address
2026-09-07 08:08:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] decoder selecting one ROM socket.
2026-09-07 08:08:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:08:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] Your README confirms the two fixed windows:
2026-09-07 08:08:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:08:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Low DOS ROM: `C000–DFFF`
2026-09-07 08:08:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] - High DOS/Kernal ROM: `E000–FFFF`
2026-09-07 08:08:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] If you are already sampling enough address bits to reconstruct the full 16-bit 6502 address, then
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] you can compute whether that ROM socket is selected:
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] ROM selected  <=>  address is inside the window of that ROM
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] So `CS` is derived information, not a critical extra bit to sample. You can free that GPIO and
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] connect it to the 6502’s SYNC output instead.
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] What to change
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Move the wire currently on GPIO26 from the ROM socket `CS` pin to the 6502 `SYNC` pin.
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Keep all address/data/RW wiring as it is.
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] - In `pinStamp()`, treat GPIO26 as `sync`, not `cs`.
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] - If you still want to print or filter by ROM selection, derive it from the 16-bit address after
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] decoding.
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] Why SYNC is the only sane answer
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] 6502 SYNC is asserted during the opcode fetch cycle. That is the clean marker for:
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] this data byte is an opcode
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] Without it, operand bytes and data bytes look exactly like opcode fetches on the bus. Every
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] possible byte value is also a valid 6502 opcode if you include undocumented opcodes, so a heuristic
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] based on “does this byte look like a valid opcode?” cannot work in general.
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] You can get away without SYNC only if you are doing offline disassembly from a known reset vector.
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] That is a different operation: you reconstruct a listing from the ROM image, not from the live bus
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] trace. And even then, one branch or interrupt that you mispredict will desynchronise the rest of
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] the stream.
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] With SYNC wired to GPIO26, each trace row can carry:
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] address data R/W S timestamp
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] where `S=1` means “instruction fetch starts here.”
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] Practical suggestion while rewiring
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] The trace you posted has a lot of duplicate/transitional samples. Before doing instruction-boundary
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] analysis, filter the samples so that each stable bus state is represented once. SYNC itself will
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] then mark much cleaner instruction starts.
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] Also, the current code drop still has the old `stringify()` signature/argument mismatch:
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] `updateRPC()` calls `stringify(s)` while `stringify()` is declared with no parameter and references
2026-09-07 08:08:01 [deepseek-v4-flash] an undefined `text`. That source file won’t compile as pasted.
