2026-09-07 08:25:00 [gemini-3.8-flash] Hello!
2026-09-07 08:25:00 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:25:00 [gemini-3.8-flash] From a hardware and transport perspective, RP2040 FIFOs (both PIO FIFOs and DMA queues) are
2026-09-07 08:25:00 [gemini-3.8-flash] strictly first-in, first-out. Hardware will not swap or transpose entries in transit.
2026-09-07 08:25:00 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:25:00 [gemini-3.8-flash] However, captured data can frequently appear out of order or corrupted due to several
2026-09-07 08:25:00 [gemini-3.8-flash] subtle software and state issues:
2026-09-07 08:25:00 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:25:00 [gemini-3.8-flash] 1. Phase Shift / Word Misalignment (Most Common)
2026-09-07 08:25:00 [gemini-3.8-flash] In your capture routine, each sample consists of two 32-bit words:
2026-09-07 08:25:00 [gemini-3.8-flash] • `captureBuffer[i * 2]` (tick)
2026-09-07 08:25:00 [gemini-3.8-flash] • `captureBuffer[i * 2 + 1]` (pins)
2026-09-07 08:25:00 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:25:00 [gemini-3.8-flash] If the PIO or DMA gets out of phase by a single 32-bit word, the interpretation alternates: the
2026-09-07 08:25:00 [gemini-3.8-flash] code will read pins as ticks and ticks as pins. This leads to erratic timestamp calculations that
2026-09-07 08:25:00 [gemini-3.8-flash] mimic severe out-of-order events. This often happens if:
2026-09-07 08:25:00 [gemini-3.8-flash] • A previous capture was aborted halfway through a tick/pin push pair.
2026-09-07 08:25:01 [gemini-3.8-flash] • The PIO RX FIFO was not completely drained or cleared before arming DMA.
2026-09-07 08:25:01 [gemini-3.8-flash] • The PIO state machine began execution and pushed one word before the DMA channel was triggered.
2026-09-07 08:25:01 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:25:01 [gemini-3.8-flash] 2. FIFO Overrun (Dropped Samples)
2026-09-07 08:25:01 [gemini-3.8-flash] If the host CPU or DMA cannot drain the PIO FIFO quickly enough and the PIO pushes non-blockingly,
2026-09-07 08:25:01 [gemini-3.8-flash] entries are discarded. If an odd number of words is dropped, this immediately creates the
2026-09-07 08:25:01 [gemini-3.8-flash] 1-word phase shift described above for all subsequent samples.
2026-09-07 08:25:01 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:25:01 [gemini-3.8-flash] 3. Down-Counting Timers
2026-09-07 08:25:01 [gemini-3.8-flash] If the PIO generates `tick` values using an internal loop counter (for example, `mov x, ...`
2026-09-07 08:25:01 [gemini-3.8-flash] followed by `jmp x--`), the counter decrements rather than increments. If interpreted directly as
2026-09-07 08:25:01 [gemini-3.8-flash] elapsed time without subtracting from a base value, subsequent samples will show decreasing
2026-09-07 08:25:01 [gemini-3.8-flash] timestamps.
2026-09-07 08:25:01 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:25:01 [gemini-3.8-flash] 4. Leftover Data Across Sessions
2026-09-07 08:25:01 [gemini-3.8-flash] If `beginSnoop()` does not explicitly restart the PIO state machine clock divider and flush the RX
2026-09-07 08:25:01 [gemini-3.8-flash] FIFO (`pio_sm_clear_fifos`), leftover data from an earlier run will be DMA'd first, creating an
2026-09-07 08:25:01 [gemini-3.8-flash] apparent time jump or interleaving old and new captures.
2026-09-07 08:25:01 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:25:01 [gemini-3.8-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-07 08:25:01 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:25:01 [gemini-3.8-flash] To diagnose this, checking whether timestamps are jumping erratically between pin masks and actual
2026-09-07 08:25:01 [gemini-3.8-flash] tick numbers is usually the quickest way to confirm a 1-word alignment slip.
2026-09-07 08:25:01 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
