2026-09-07 08:27:44 [gemini-3.8-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 gemini-3.8-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.018 19.64KB 12.63s
2026-09-07 08:27:44 [gemini-3.8-flash] Several interesting aspects and subtle details stand out across the shared files:
2026-09-07 08:27:44 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:27:44 [gemini-3.8-flash] 1. Full 16-bit Address Decoding via CPU Tap
2026-09-07 08:27:44 [gemini-3.8-flash] Because the 2364 ROM socket (UB3) only routes A_0 through A_12, the additional lines for A_13 \dots
2026-09-07 08:27:44 [gemini-3.8-flash] A_15 and R/\overlineW routed directly from the 6502 pins allow full 64\,\textKB bus monitoring
2026-09-07 08:27:44 [gemini-3.8-flash] rather than just observing local ROM chip accesses. Reversing A_0 \dots A_7 in `pinStamp()` with
2026-09-07 08:27:44 [gemini-3.8-flash] `reverse8()` aligns neatly with the physical pin order along that side of the 2364 socket.
2026-09-07 08:27:44 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:27:44 [gemini-3.8-flash] 2. PIO State Machine (`snoop.pio`)
2026-09-07 08:27:44 [gemini-3.8-flash] • State Preservation Across Pushes: Using register `x` as a temporary holder for the tick
2026-09-07 08:27:44 [gemini-3.8-flash] counter before pushing to the RX FIFO works around the hardware behavior where manual `push`
2026-09-07 08:27:44 [gemini-3.8-flash] instructions clear the ISR.
2026-09-07 08:27:44 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Sampling Rate: The loop executes a `nop [8]` before reading the pins, giving a consistent
2026-09-07 08:27:44 [gemini-3.8-flash] sampling cadence while monitoring bus transactions. If higher bus frequencies or narrow transition
2026-09-07 08:27:44 [gemini-3.8-flash] windows ever need to be captured, that delay can be adjusted down to match the 1541's \approx
2026-09-07 08:27:44 [gemini-3.8-flash] 1\,\textMHz bus clock \phi_2.
2026-09-07 08:27:44 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:27:44 [gemini-3.8-flash] 3. Implementation Details in `snoop.cpp`
2026-09-07 08:27:44 [gemini-3.8-flash] A few items in the C++ layer may warrant a quick check depending on your build configuration:
2026-09-07 08:27:44 [gemini-3.8-flash] • `fifo` Scope: In `beginSnoop()`, `fifo` is assigned directly without a local type
2026-09-07 08:27:44 [gemini-3.8-flash] declaration (`fifo = &snoop_pio->rxf[snoop_sm];`). Unless defined in a shared header or globally,
2026-09-07 08:27:44 [gemini-3.8-flash] this may require an explicit pointer type.
2026-09-07 08:27:44 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Unused Configuration Structure: `initSnoop()` initializes `pio_sm_config c` and then
2026-09-07 08:27:44 [gemini-3.8-flash] immediately creates a second `pio_sm_config config` that is never applied to the state machine.
2026-09-07 08:27:44 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Pin Direction Setup: While `pio_gpio_init()` assigns the pad control over to the PIO
2026-09-07 08:27:44 [gemini-3.8-flash] instance, explicitly calling `pio_sm_set_consecutive_pindirs()` ensures the state machine input
2026-09-07 08:27:44 [gemini-3.8-flash] mapping is explicitly configured for input.
2026-09-07 08:27:44 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:27:44 [gemini-3.8-flash] 4. Hardware Expansion (SWD Tail)
2026-09-07 08:27:44 [gemini-3.8-flash] The note in the documentation regarding using the SWD pads for additional GPIO on the RP2040 (GPIO
2026-09-07 08:27:44 [gemini-3.8-flash] 30 and 31) is a practical choice once the 28 standard header pins are fully allocated to the bus
2026-09-07 08:27:44 [gemini-3.8-flash] lines.
2026-09-07 08:27:44 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:27:44 [gemini-3.8-flash] If you would like closer analysis on the DMA pacing, RPC packet serialization, or the PIO
2026-09-07 08:27:44 [gemini-3.8-flash] transition timing relative to the 6502 clock phases, please let me know.
