2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 gemini-3.8-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.017 23.40KB 11.19s
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] There are three specific issues with how the DMA and samples are handled that can cause subtle
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] bugs:
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] 1. FIFO Leftovers and Pair Alignment
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] In `initSnoop()`, the state machine is enabled immediately (`pio_sm_set_enabled`), so the PIO
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] begins running and pushing words to the RX FIFO before capture starts.
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] If any pin changes occur before `beginSnoop()` is called, up to 8 words will already be sitting in
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] the joined FIFO. When the DMA starts:
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Old pre-trigger events are transferred first.
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] • If an odd number of words was sitting in the FIFO, the stream becomes desynchronized: every
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] tick will be read as pins, and every pins will be read as a tick.
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] Draining or resetting the FIFO in `beginSnoop()` prior to arming the DMA avoids this.
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] `pio_sm_clear_fifos(snoop_pio, snoop_sm)` handles this flush cleanly.
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] 2. Sample Count Calculation on Partial Captures
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] In `getCapturedSampleCount()`:
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] return snoopRequested - (dma_channel_hw_addr(snoop_dma_chan)->transfer_count / 2);
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] Because integer division truncates down, an odd number of remaining words produces an off-by-one
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] error.
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] For example, if exactly 1 word (a single tick without its pins) has arrived:
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] • `transfer_count` is 8191
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] • 8191 / 2 = 4095
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] • 4096 - 4095 = 1
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] The function reports 1 completed sample even though only half of it is present in memory, causing
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] `captureBuffer[1]` to read uninitialized memory.
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] Calculating from the words actually transferred resolves the rounding:
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] uint32_t remaining = dma_channel_hw_addr(snoop_dma_chan)->transfer_count;
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] uint32_t transferred = (snoopRequested * 2) - remaining;
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] return transferred / 2;
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:30:45 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
