2026-09-07 08:38:15 [deepseek-v4-flash] A 6502 SYNC pulse is what tells you “the data byte on this bus cycle is an opcode.” There is no
2026-09-07 08:38:15 [deepseek-v4-flash] general way to recover that from address/data/RW alone because:
2026-09-07 08:38:15 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:38:15 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Every 8-bit value is also a valid opcode if you include undocumented 6502 opcodes.
2026-09-07 08:38:15 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Operand bytes, table reads, and stack reads look identical to opcode fetches on the bus.
2026-09-07 08:38:15 [deepseek-v4-flash] - A branch or interrupt will desynchronise any stream-based guess.
2026-09-07 08:38:15 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:38:15 [deepseek-v4-flash] So “infer sync bit in C++” is not something I would design around.
