2026-09-07 08:39:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] 1. The Decrementing Tick Order and Identical Timestamps
2026-09-07 08:39:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:39:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] The tick values are actually arriving in chronological order:
2026-09-07 08:39:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:39:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Countdown Counter: In `snoop.pio`, the tick is seeded with `mov isr, ~null`
2026-09-07 08:39:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] (`0xFFFFFFFF`) and decremented using `jmp x--, save`. Higher numerical values represent earlier
2026-09-07 08:39:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] points in time. Therefore, `0xed32f9` occurred before `0xed32f3` by exactly 6 loop iterations.
2026-09-07 08:39:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Identical Timestamps on Rapid Changes: In `snoop.pio`, when the state machine branches to
2026-09-07 08:39:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] `changed`, it pushes the current `isr` and jumps directly back to `loop` without executing the `jmp
2026-09-07 08:39:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] x--, save` decrement step. If pins are settling and register another change immediately on the
2026-09-07 08:39:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] subsequent pass, `isr` retains the exact same value.
2026-09-07 08:39:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:39:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-07 08:39:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:39:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] 2. Detecting Settling and Jitter
2026-09-07 08:39:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:39:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] On a 6502 bus, address lines propagate through internal decoders and external buffers with varying
2026-09-07 08:39:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] propagation delays (often skewing across a 30–100 ns window).
2026-09-07 08:39:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:39:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] The trace reflects this: `4c47` versus `4847` is bit 10 toggling while the bus stabilizes.
2026-09-07 08:39:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:39:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] Handling in PIO (Hardware Filtering)
2026-09-07 08:39:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] If you do not have a free pin to sample \phi_2 directly:
2026-09-07 08:39:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Instead of immediately pushing upon the first detection of `x != y`, the PIO routine can execute
2026-09-07 08:39:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] a short settling delay (for example, 150–250 ns).
2026-09-07 08:39:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] • After the delay, it samples `pins` again. If the bus has stabilized, it pushes that single
2026-09-07 08:39:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] consolidated value and ignores the intermediate transitions.
2026-09-07 08:39:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:39:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] Handling in C++ (Software Filtering)
2026-09-07 08:39:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] Because bus settling occurs across single-digit tick deltas:
2026-09-07 08:39:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] • A reusable filter function can process the raw capture buffer into an intermediate settled event
2026-09-07 08:39:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] buffer.
2026-09-07 08:39:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] • When adjacent samples fall within a settling threshold (\Delta t \le \textsettle\_limit), earlier
2026-09-07 08:39:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] transient samples are discarded, keeping only the final state before the bus went quiet.
2026-09-07 08:39:20 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:39:20 [gemini-3.8-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-07 08:39:20 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:39:20 [gemini-3.8-flash] 3. Inferring the 6502 `SYNC` Line in C++
2026-09-07 08:39:20 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:39:20 [gemini-3.8-flash] Without a physical pin for 6502 pin 7 (`SYNC`), you can infer opcode fetch cycles by running a
2026-09-07 08:39:20 [gemini-3.8-flash] lightweight bus cycle tracker:
2026-09-07 08:39:20 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 08:39:20 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Initial Synchronization: The 6502 boot sequence always reads the reset vector from `FFFC`
2026-09-07 08:39:20 [gemini-3.8-flash] and `FFFD` (with R/\overlineW = 1). Once the initial program counter (PC) is loaded, the subsequent
2026-09-07 08:39:20 [gemini-3.8-flash] read cycle at that target address is guaranteed to be an opcode fetch (`SYNC = 1`).
2026-09-07 08:39:20 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Cycle State Machine: Every 6502 opcode has a deterministic cycle length and memory access
2026-09-07 08:39:20 [gemini-3.8-flash] sequence (e.g., fetch opcode \rightarrow fetch operand low \rightarrow fetch operand high
2026-09-07 08:39:20 [gemini-3.8-flash] \rightarrow internal operation / memory access).
2026-09-07 08:39:20 [gemini-3.8-flash] • PC Tracking: By tracking the decoded instruction, the state machine knows how many bus
2026-09-07 08:39:20 [gemini-3.8-flash] cycles to wait before the next opcode fetch occurs. Branches, jumps, interrupts, and subroutines
2026-09-07 08:39:20 [gemini-3.8-flash] update the expected PC, keeping the synthetic `SYNC` flag aligned.
2026-09-07 08:39:20 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
