2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] 1. Bus Settling and Jitter in PIO
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] The multiple records observed with identical or adjacent timestamps are the result of signal skew
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] across physical traces and logic gate propagation delays. Because the PIO loop executes at system
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] clock speed (typically 125 MHz to 133 MHz, or ~7.5–8 ns per cycle), it captures the intermediate
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] settling states as the 6502 address and control lines transition.
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] • PIO-side Debounce / Delay:
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash]   Rather than pushing immediately upon `x != y`, the PIO program can insert a brief settling delay.
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] After detecting an initial change, the state machine can execute a fixed wait loop or delay counter
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] (for example, 40–80 ns) and then sample the bus once more before pushing to the FIFO.
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Clock Qualification (Φ2):
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash]   On the 6502 bus (running at ~1 MHz in the 1541), address lines become stable approximately
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] 100–300 ns after the rising edge of phase 1, and read data is valid near the falling edge of phase
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] 2 (\Phi_2). If one of the unused GPIOs or an external latch is tied to \Phi_2, sampling can be
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] triggered strictly on the falling edge of \Phi_2 using `wait 1 gpio ...` / `wait 0 gpio ...`,
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] entirely bypassing settling transients.
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] 2. Settling Filter in C++
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] If bus capture remains continuous without PIO qualification, filtering is straightforward during
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] post-processing or stream consumption:
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Cluster Windowing:
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash]   Because the 1541 bus runs at 1 MHz (1\,μ\texts per bus cycle), any series of raw samples arriving
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] within a narrow threshold (e.g., within 10–20 PIO clock ticks of each other) represent the same
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] transition event.
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Last-Wins Strategy:
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash]   A dedicated filter function can group samples by timestamp delta. For any cluster where
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] consecutive timestamps differ by less than the settling threshold, all transient intermediate
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] states can be discarded, keeping only the final sample in the cluster as the resolved cycle value.
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] 3. Inferring the SYNC Signal in Software
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] Without the physical SYNC pin (6502 pin 7) connected to a GPIO:
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Vector Seeding:
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash]   Following a reset sequence, the 6502 executes seven internal cycles, reading the reset vector
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] from `FFFC` and `FFFD`. The word retrieved is the program counter (PC) for the first instruction.
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Instruction Decoder State Machine:
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash]   By implementing a small table lookup based on standard 6502 instruction lengths and addressing
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] modes, the C++ processor can track instruction boundaries:
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash]   1. The byte read at the current PC is flagged as an opcode fetch (`SYNC = 1`).
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash]   2. The table yields the expected operand byte count and instruction cycle behavior.
2026-09-07 09:55:48 [gemini-3.8-flash]   3. Subsequent bus reads within that instruction window are flagged as operand/data fetches (`SYNC
2026-09-07 09:55:49 [gemini-3.8-flash] = 0`).
2026-09-07 09:55:49 [gemini-3.8-flash]   4. The internal PC advances according to the instruction rules, including tracking relative
2026-09-07 09:55:49 [gemini-3.8-flash] branches and absolute jumps, keeping the software in lockstep with the hardware execution stream.
2026-09-07 09:55:49 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-07 09:55:49 [gemini-3.8-flash] Let me know if you would like dedicated PIO settling routines or the C++ bus-filtering and
2026-09-07 09:55:49 [gemini-3.8-flash] instruction-tracking functions written out.
