2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] Hi Simon / nitrologic,
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] Thanks for sharing the latest romulon files. A few observations on what’s standing out now versus
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] earlier versions, plus some things worth tightening:
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] • What’s new / different*
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] - The PIO-based capture path is now the primary sampler (`snoopRom`), replacing or superseding the
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] older `sampleRom` bit-bash loop. That’s a big step — much higher bandwidth and lower CPU
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] involvement.
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] - Timestamps are now derived from the PIO countdown counter and rendered as `epoch - tick`, giving
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] positive monotonic deltas. The trace excerpts in `thoughts.txt` look coherent: roughly 80–90 ns per
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] count at 125 MHz.
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] - The JSON-RPC surface has been simplified to a small set of methods (`rtc.set`, `vidbit.set`,
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] `vidbit.keys`) and a result-pushing channel for samples.
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] • Things I’d flag*
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] 1. `int id = 2e5 + (sendCount++);` — `2e5` is a `double` literal. It happens to truncate to
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] `200000`, but it’s a needless trap. Use `200000`.
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] 2. `memcmp(_nvm, nvm_header, sizeof(nonVolatile))` compares 24 bytes starting from a pointer to the
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] string literal `"PICOTOOL"`. The literal is only 9 bytes including the NUL, so this reads past it,
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] and it also compares padding/flags. Better: `memcmp(_nvm->header, nvm_header, 8) == 0`.
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] 3. The PIO program uses `x` for two jobs: countdown timer and temporary pin sample. The
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] restore-via-ISR trick works, but it means:
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code]    - Time spent in the “changed” push path is not counted, so long bursts of transitions drift
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] slightly.
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code]    - Once `x` decrements to zero, `jmp x--, count` stops jumping and `x` stays at zero forever,
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] freezing the timer. At 125 MHz that’s ~343 seconds of idle bus, so it may not bite you in practice,
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] but it is a real landmine for long captures.
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] 4. There’s no debounce/settle filtering yet, which matches what you already noted in
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] `thoughts.txt`. The trace excerpts with intermediate values like `4c4b → 4c47 → 4847` are classic
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] bus skew. You can attack this either in the PIO (require N stable samples before reporting a
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] change) or in C++ (drop/merge samples whose duration is below a glitch threshold).
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] 5. `beginSnoop` does not reset the PIO state machine or drain stale FIFO data before starting a new
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] capture. If you ever restart capture after a partial run, the first samples could be leftovers.
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] Consider clearing the FIFO and restarting the SM program counter before arming DMA.
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] 6. `std::endl` in `rpcSend` flushes the stream every single sample. For 4096 samples that’s a lot
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] of USB overhead. Use `'\n'` and rely on the periodic `cdcFlush()` calls you already have in the
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] sample loop.
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] 7. `updateRPC` calls `parser.parseJSON` but never checks the return value before using `payload`. A
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] malformed line will likely crash. Check the parse status and guard `payload`.
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] 8. `rpcSend` takes `std::string` by value. For hot paths, `const std::string&` removes copies.
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] 9. `sampleRom` looks like dead code now — left over from before PIO. If it’s not used, consider
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] removing it to reduce confusion.
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] 10. `runShell` only arms `snoopRom` once after `vidbit.set` because `snoopReady` is never set true
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] again. If you want recurring captures, you’ll need to reset `snoopReady` somewhere or change the
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] trigger model.
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] 11. The pin-map comment says `A7..A0 | 5..12`, and `pinStamp` does `reverse8((pins>>5)&0xff)`. Make
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] sure the reversal matches your physical wiring — it’s easy for A0/A7 to end up swapped.
2026-09-09 08:40:59 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-09 08:41:00 [kimi-k2.7-code] 12. The address mask in `runShell` (`0x02000007`) ignores A13–A15 plus the LED pin. That’s fine for
2026-09-09 08:41:00 [kimi-k2.7-code] suppressing noise, but be aware it means high-address transitions alone won’t wake the shell loop.
2026-09-09 08:41:00 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-09 08:41:00 [kimi-k2.7-code] • Overall*
2026-09-09 08:41:00 [kimi-k2.7-code] The architecture feels right: PIO does the heavy lifting, the shell loop stays responsive, and
2026-09-09 08:41:00 [kimi-k2.7-code] samples stream out as JSON-RPC. The two biggest wins from here are probably adding glitch filtering
2026-09-09 08:41:00 [kimi-k2.7-code] for the skew you’re already seeing, and making `snoopRom` robust for repeated captures by resetting
2026-09-09 08:41:00 [kimi-k2.7-code] state each time.
2026-09-09 08:41:00 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-09 08:41:00 [kimi-k2.7-code] Let me know if you want me to sketch out a PIO debouncer or a C++ sample-merging filter.
