2026-09-09 16:27:28 [kimi-k2.7-code] Hi — here are some observations on the current `romulon` code, no patches unless you want them.
2026-09-09 16:27:28 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-09 16:27:28 [kimi-k2.7-code] Big-picture
2026-09-09 16:27:28 [kimi-k2.7-code] This is a Pico-based 6502-bus sampler: PIO watches GPIOs, DMA pushes `(tick, pins)` pairs into a
2026-09-09 16:27:28 [kimi-k2.7-code] buffer, and the main loop streams formatted samples out over USB CDC as JSON-RPC. The decoding in
2026-09-09 16:27:28 [kimi-k2.7-code] `pinStamp()` maps the raw Pico GPIOs back to 6502 address/data/RW/CS lines.
2026-09-09 16:27:28 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-09 16:27:28 [kimi-k2.7-code] Things that look like they need fixing
2026-09-09 16:27:28 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] 1. `pinBuffer` is never sized, so `snoopLog()` probably sees zero samples
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code]    - In `snoopRom()` you `reserve(MAX_SAMPLES)` then take `pinBuffer.data()`, but `reserve()` does
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] not change `size()`.
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code]    - `snoopLog()` reads `pinBuffer.size()`, which is still `0`.
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code]    - The DMA will overwrite the allocated storage, but the vector does not know about it.
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code]    - Fix: `pinBuffer.resize(MAX_SAMPLES)` before starting DMA, or track the sample count
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] separately.
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] 2. `snoopLog()` can underflow when `n == 0`
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code]    - `n = pinBuffer.size(); if (n > 128) n = 128;` then `for (i = 0; i < n - 1; i++)`.
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code]    - If `n == 0`, `n - 1` wraps to `SIZE_MAX` and the loop runs out of bounds.
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code]    - Guard with `if (n < 2) return;`.
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] 3. `beginSnoop()` DMA transfer count is `max_samples * 2`
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code]    - That matches the `(tick, pins)` pairs, which is fine as long as `max_samples` really means
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] “number of `GPIOSample` entries.”
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code]    - Just make sure the caller passes the sample count, not the raw `uint32_t` word count.
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] 4. `rpcSend` id uses a double literal
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code]    - `int id = 2e5 + (sendCount++);` — `2e5` has type `double`. It will truncate, but it’s worth
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] changing to `200000`.
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] 5. JSON parse result is not checked
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code]    - `parser.parseJSON(line, &payload)` returns a status, but you dereference `payload`
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] unconditionally.
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code]    - If parsing fails, this will crash.
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] 6. `std::endl` after every JSON line
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code]    - Each `out << ... << std::endl;` flushes the stream. Over CDC that can be expensive.
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code]    - Prefer `'\n'` and flush explicitly when you need to (you already call `cdcFlush()`).
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] 7. `runShell()` watchdog update is conditional on `!bootSel`
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code]    - If `readBootSelect()` ever returns `true` for a long time, the watchdog could fire.
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code]    - Usually you want to `watchdog_update()` on every healthy loop iteration.
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] Smaller / stylistic notes
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] - `pinStamp()` uses a fixed static `stampBuffer[20]` and returns a `std::string` constructed from
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] it. That’s not thread-safe, but on a single-core Pico it’s probably fine. Still, a local buffer or
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] direct construction would be cleaner.
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] - Lots of globals are declared but unused (`currentPins`, `currentCount`, `blinkCount`, `rtcGood`,
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] `mode`, `dpins`, `apins`, `hitCount`, `commandQueue`, `shellActive`, `emitBuffer`). Not a bug, but
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] it makes it harder to see what’s actually wired up.
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] - `stringify()` always `reserve(1024)` regardless of input length; `reserve(text.size() * 2 + 2)`
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] would be closer to reality.
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] PIO sampler
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] The PIO loop is simple and should work for detecting pin changes. A couple of things to keep in
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] mind:
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] - The counter `x` decrements continuously, so the “timestamp” is really a relative cycle count, not
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] wall time. Wrapping is fine for deltas as long as you compare consecutive samples.
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] - With the current delay slot and NOP, you’re sampling GPIO roughly every ~9–10 PIO cycles (~70–80
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] ns at 125 MHz). That’s fast enough for a 1 MHz 6502, but multiple rapid transitions within that
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] window will be merged into the first observed change.
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] Overall
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] The architecture is clear, but the `pinBuffer` sizing bug means the logging path is probably not
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] doing what you think it’s doing right now. Fixing `reserve` → `resize` and guarding the `n - 1`
2026-09-09 16:27:29 [kimi-k2.7-code] loop are the two highest-impact changes.
