2026-09-09 16:58:45 [grok-build-0.1] Here's my honest take on the romulon codebase after reading through it.
2026-09-09 16:58:45 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 16:58:45 [grok-build-0.1] Overall Impression
2026-09-09 16:58:45 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 16:58:45 [grok-build-0.1] This is a classic "make the Pico do something it wasn't really designed for" project — using PIO +
2026-09-09 16:58:45 [grok-build-0.1] DMA to snoop a 6502 bus at decent speed. The core idea is sound, and you're getting real data out,
2026-09-09 16:58:45 [grok-build-0.1] which is the important part. However, the implementation has several fragile areas that are likely
2026-09-09 16:58:45 [grok-build-0.1] to bite you as you try to improve capture quality or add features.
2026-09-09 16:58:45 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 16:58:45 [grok-build-0.1] Critical Issues
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] • 1. Dangerous buffer handling (biggest red flag)*
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] uint32_t* buffer = (uint32_t*)(pinBuffer.data());
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] beginSnoop(buffer, MAX_SAMPLES);
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] You're casting a `std::vector<GPIOSample>` directly to a `uint32_t` and letting DMA write into
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] it. This works right now* because `GPIOSample` is two `uint32_t`s, but it's undefined behavior
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] territory. Any reallocation, or even some vector operations, can invalidate the pointer. You should
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] either:
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] - Use a raw array + manual tracking, or
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] - Use a `std::vector<uint32_t>` and reinterpret the data later.
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] • 2. PIO timestamp mechanism is brittle*
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] You're using the PIO's `x` register as a free-running down-counter. This creates several problems:
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] - Timestamps go backwards (hence the `epoch - tick` dance).
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] - Resolution and wrap behavior are awkward.
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] - The way you're pushing two words (timestamp + pins) on every change has some structural issues in
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] the PIO program.
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] The current `snoop.pio` is trying to do too much in one program. The `jmp x--, count` + `nop [4]`
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] pattern is a bit of a hack for timing.
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] • 3. Missing definitions*
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] Several functions are used but not present in the files you shared:
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] - `hexout()`
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] - `cdcReadLine()`, `cdcFlush()`, `cdcStatus()`, `cdcInit()`
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] - `out` stream
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] - `setTime()`, `readBootSelect()`, etc.
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] This makes it hard to fully review the USB/CDC side.
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] Design Observations
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] ╭──────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬──────────╮
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] │  Area                        │  Comment                                                                                        │  Risk    │
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] ├──────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────┤
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] │  **Address reconstruction**  │  `reverse8()` + manual bit shuffling is error-prone and hard to maintain                        │  Medium  │
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] │  **Filtering**               │  Doing basic delta filtering in `snoopLog()` is fine, but you're still shipping a lot of noise  │  Low     │
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] │  **RPC protocol**            │  Very ad-hoc. Mixing different result shapes makes client parsing painful                       │  Medium  │
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] │  **Main loop**               │  Polling `gpio_get_all()` every iteration while also doing snooping is messy                    │  Medium  │
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] │  **Globals**                 │  Heavy use of globals for state (`snoopConnected`, `epoch`, etc.)                               │  Medium  │
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] ╰──────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴──────────╯
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] The `pinStamp()` function is doing too much work (bit mangling + formatting) on every sample. This
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] should probably be separated into "decode" and "format" stages.
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] PIO Program Thoughts
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] The current approach of pushing on every pin change is aggressive. You're going to get a
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] lot of settling/glitch samples (which you're already seeing in `thoughts.txt`).
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] A few directions people usually go:
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] - Capture on every change but do smarter filtering later (what you're doing)
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] - Add some hysteresis/debounce logic in the PIO itself
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] - Switch to sampling at a fixed rate instead of change detection (simpler but higher bandwidth)
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] Your current PIO also has a subtle issue: when it detects a change, it pushes the old
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] timestamp and the new pins (or vice versa). The ordering isn't super clear.
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] Questions
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 16:58:46 [grok-build-0.1] To give you more useful feedback, I need to know your priorities:
2026-09-09 16:58:47 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 16:58:47 [grok-build-0.1] 1. What is the actual goal right now?
2026-09-09 16:58:47 [grok-build-0.1]    Just getting clean traces, or do you want to do protocol decoding (detect opcodes, operands,
2026-09-09 16:58:47 [grok-build-0.1] memory accesses, etc.)?
2026-09-09 16:58:47 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 16:58:47 [grok-build-0.1] 2. How important is timing accuracy vs throughput?
2026-09-09 16:58:47 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 16:58:47 [grok-build-0.1] 3. Are you planning to keep using the current JSON-RPC-over-CDC approach, or is that just for
2026-09-09 16:58:47 [grok-build-0.1] development?
2026-09-09 16:58:47 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 16:58:47 [grok-build-0.1] 4. Do you want me to focus on any specific part? (e.g. fixing the PIO program, cleaning up the
2026-09-09 16:58:47 [grok-build-0.1] capture pipeline, making the address decoding less painful, etc.)
2026-09-09 16:58:47 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 16:58:47 [grok-build-0.1] Also, one quick tactical suggestion: stop using the vector cast trick immediately and switch to a
2026-09-09 16:58:47 [grok-build-0.1] plain `uint32_t` buffer for DMA. That's the change most likely to save you pain later.
2026-09-09 16:58:47 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 16:58:47 [grok-build-0.1] Want me to go deeper on any of these areas?
2026-09-09 16:58:47 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 17:28:31 [grok-build-0.1] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.007 19.97KB 34.80s
2026-09-09 17:28:31 [grok-build-0.1] Thank you for sharing the Romulon source. The project is a clean attempt to use RP2040 PIO + DMA
2026-09-09 17:28:31 [grok-build-0.1] for non-intrusive 6502 bus capture on a 1541, with the host side apparently being a JSON-RPC client
2026-09-09 17:28:31 [grok-build-0.1] (vidbit).
2026-09-09 17:28:31 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 17:28:31 [grok-build-0.1] High-level observations
2026-09-09 17:28:31 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 17:28:31 [grok-build-0.1] The core capture path (snoop.pio → DMA → pinBuffer) is the right shape for this kind of work. Using
2026-09-09 17:28:31 [grok-build-0.1] a free-running down-counter for relative timestamps avoids needing another timer channel. The
2026-09-09 17:28:31 [grok-build-0.1] filtering in snoopLog (delta < 20 ticks) is a reasonable first attempt at removing settling/glitch
2026-09-09 17:28:31 [grok-build-0.1] events.
2026-09-09 17:28:31 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 17:28:31 [grok-build-0.1] Notable issues
2026-09-09 17:28:31 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 17:28:31 [grok-build-0.1] - Memory budget. `MAX_SAMPLES = 1024*1024` with `GPIOSample` (8 bytes each) is 8 MiB. The
2026-09-09 17:28:31 [grok-build-0.1] RP2040 has ~264 KiB of SRAM. `pinBuffer.resize(MAX_SAMPLES)` will fail at runtime. Even 16k–32k
2026-09-09 17:28:31 [grok-build-0.1] samples is already pushing it once you account for stack, USB buffers, etc.
2026-09-09 17:28:31 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 17:28:31 [grok-build-0.1] - Captured count handling. After `snoopComplete()`, the code still walks `pinBuffer.size()`
2026-09-09 17:28:31 [grok-build-0.1] (the full allocated size) and hard-caps at 1024. It should use `getCapturedSampleCount()` and only
2026-09-09 17:28:31 [grok-build-0.1] process/resize to the actual number of valid pairs written by DMA.
2026-09-09 17:28:31 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 17:28:31 [grok-build-0.1] - Epoch and ordering. `epoch` is set lazily inside `pinStamp`. When `snoopLog` later calls
2026-09-09 17:28:31 [grok-build-0.1] it on a buffer that was captured with `epoch==0`, the first call mutates the global. This works for
2026-09-09 17:28:31 [grok-build-0.1] one-shot logs but is fragile if you ever want to log multiple captures or interleave other output.
2026-09-09 17:28:31 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 17:28:31 [grok-build-0.1] - PIO counter behaviour. The down-counter + `nop [4]` gives a coarse but usable tick. The
2026-09-09 17:28:31 [grok-build-0.1] current filtering assumes the first of a close pair is the "real" value. You may want to keep the
2026-09-09 17:28:31 [grok-build-0.1] last of a settling burst instead (or both with a flag).
2026-09-09 17:28:31 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 17:28:31 [grok-build-0.1] - Trigger path. The polling loop with `sleep_us(1)` for the CS trigger is acceptable for a
2026-09-09 17:28:31 [grok-build-0.1] one-time arm, but it burns cycles and can miss the exact edge if the mask is broad.
2026-09-09 17:28:31 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 17:28:31 [grok-build-0.1] - Missing pieces. Several symbols (`hexout`, the cdc* layer, `out`, `JSONParser`,
2026-09-09 17:28:31 [grok-build-0.1] `setTime`, `readBootSelect`, watchdog wrappers) are referenced but not present in the shared files.
2026-09-09 17:28:31 [grok-build-0.1] I assume they live in picosdk/ or other .cpp units.
2026-09-09 17:28:32 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 17:28:32 [grok-build-0.1] Smaller notes
2026-09-09 17:28:32 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 17:28:32 [grok-build-0.1] - `reverse8` is only used for A0–A7. The rest of the address reconstruction is done with shifts and
2026-09-09 17:28:32 [grok-build-0.1] masks. This is fine, but the mapping is now spread across `pinStamp` and the pin table comment.
2026-09-09 17:28:32 [grok-build-0.1] - `snoopLog` only ever sends the first 1024 qualifying samples even after a large capture. If the
2026-09-09 17:28:32 [grok-build-0.1] intent is "send everything that survived filtering," the 1024 cap should probably be removed or
2026-09-09 17:28:32 [grok-build-0.1] made a parameter.
2026-09-09 17:28:32 [grok-build-0.1] - The JSON-RPC framing writes directly to `out` with `std::endl`. On a USB CDC link this can
2026-09-09 17:28:32 [grok-build-0.1] produce a lot of small packets.
2026-09-09 17:28:32 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 17:28:32 [grok-build-0.1] If you have a specific goal for the next step (smaller reliable capture buffer, better settling
2026-09-09 17:28:32 [grok-build-0.1] filter, timestamp format change, trigger improvements, host-side trace decoder, etc.), let me know
2026-09-09 17:28:32 [grok-build-0.1] and I can give targeted suggestions or sketch the shape of a helper function.
2026-09-09 17:28:32 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 17:29:47 [grok-build-0.1] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.011 33.85KB 27.70s
2026-09-09 17:29:47 [grok-build-0.1] Thank you for the update.
2026-09-09 17:29:47 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 17:29:47 [grok-build-0.1] The reduction of `MAX_SAMPLES` to 4096 is a clear improvement and now fits comfortably in RP2040
2026-09-09 17:29:47 [grok-build-0.1] SRAM.
2026-09-09 17:29:47 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 17:29:47 [grok-build-0.1] Remaining issues worth addressing
2026-09-09 17:29:47 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 17:29:47 [grok-build-0.1] • Actual captured length is ignored*
2026-09-09 17:29:47 [grok-build-0.1] After `snoopComplete()` returns, the code still treats the entire `pinBuffer` (size 4096) as valid
2026-09-09 17:29:47 [grok-build-0.1] data. The DMA only fills as many pairs as actually occurred. `getCapturedSampleCount()` exists for
2026-09-09 17:29:47 [grok-build-0.1] this reason but is not used after the capture completes. As a result, `snoopLog()` walks past the
2026-09-09 17:29:47 [grok-build-0.1] real data and the 1024 hard cap inside it becomes the only thing limiting output.
2026-09-09 17:29:47 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 17:29:47 [grok-build-0.1] • Epoch handling across multiple captures*
2026-09-09 17:29:47 [grok-build-0.1] `epoch` is set once on the first call to `pinStamp` and then never updated. Because the PIO counter
2026-09-09 17:29:47 [grok-build-0.1] keeps running between captures, a second `snoopRom` + `snoopLog` will produce incorrect deltas in
2026-09-09 17:29:47 [grok-build-0.1] `pinStamp`. Each capture run should establish its own reference point from the data it just
2026-09-09 17:29:47 [grok-build-0.1] received.
2026-09-09 17:29:47 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 17:29:47 [grok-build-0.1] • Filtering and logging limits*
2026-09-09 17:29:47 [grok-build-0.1] `snoopLog()` still forces `n = min(n, 1024)` and then applies the `< 20` delta filter. With the new
2026-09-09 17:29:47 [grok-build-0.1] smaller buffer size, the 1024 cap is less necessary, but the logic still doesn't use the real
2026-09-09 17:29:47 [grok-build-0.1] number of samples returned by the DMA.
2026-09-09 17:29:47 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 17:29:47 [grok-build-0.1] • Buffer ownership after DMA*
2026-09-09 17:29:47 [grok-build-0.1] The vector is resized to `MAX_SAMPLES` before capture, then the DMA writes directly into it as raw
2026-09-09 17:29:47 [grok-build-0.1] `uint32_t` pairs. After capture it would be cleaner to trim the vector down to the actual number of
2026-09-09 17:29:47 [grok-build-0.1] complete samples before any processing or logging.
2026-09-09 17:29:47 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 17:29:47 [grok-build-0.1] • Trigger path*
2026-09-09 17:29:47 [grok-build-0.1] The busy-wait with `sleep_us(1)` for the CS trigger is still present. It works but occupies the
2026-09-09 17:29:48 [grok-build-0.1] core during arming.
2026-09-09 17:29:48 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 17:29:48 [grok-build-0.1] Other observations
2026-09-09 17:29:48 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 17:29:48 [grok-build-0.1] - The live pin polling loop in `runShell` and the one-shot snoop path are still tightly coupled
2026-09-09 17:29:48 [grok-build-0.1] through the `snoopConnected` / `snoopReady` flags. That design is understandable for the current
2026-09-09 17:29:48 [grok-build-0.1] host interaction model.
2026-09-09 17:29:48 [grok-build-0.1] - `pinStamp` still does all the address reconstruction inline. The bit mapping comments at the top
2026-09-09 17:29:48 [grok-build-0.1] are helpful but the reconstruction logic is spread out.
2026-09-09 17:29:48 [grok-build-0.1] - Yielding with `watchdog_update`, `tud_task`, and `cdcFlush` every 8 logged lines during
2026-09-09 17:29:48 [grok-build-0.1] `snoopLog` is still a reasonable way to keep USB responsive.
2026-09-09 17:29:48 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 17:29:48 [grok-build-0.1] If you would like to focus on any of the above (especially proper use of captured count,
2026-09-09 17:29:48 [grok-build-0.1] per-capture epoch, or trimming the buffer after DMA), let me know which area to discuss in more
2026-09-09 17:29:48 [grok-build-0.1] detail.
