2026-09-09 17:33:03 [grok-build-0.1] Thank you for the trace.
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] Observations from the log
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] - The capture begins with a long run of `$0000,00,W,0` entries. These have very large timestamp
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] deltas at first and then continue with smaller ones. This pattern is almost certainly not real ROM
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] traffic — it looks like either floating bus conditions, periods when the monitored ROM is not
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] selected, or address reconstruction showing 0 when the upper address bits are not being driven to
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] the expected values.
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] - There is a short burst around `BF89` and `0080`/`$0081` with mixed CS states.
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] - The trace then settles into a very regular alternating pattern:
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1]   - `EAAD` reading `95`
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1]   - `EAB0` reading `D0`
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1]   - All marked as reads (`R`) with CS=`1`
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1]   The timestamps keep incrementing steadily. This is classic 6502 tight-loop polling behaviour —
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] the CPU is repeatedly executing LDA instructions to these two absolute addresses, most likely
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] waiting on a status flag, disk controller, or serial bus state.
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] - Importantly, the long polling loop shows CS=`1` (inactive) for every sample. If the goal is to
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] capture data from the ROM chip whose /CE you wired to GPIO 26, these accesses are not coming from
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] that ROM. They are either from the other ROM or from RAM.
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] Immediate problems visible in the current capture path
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] 1. `snoopLog` still ignores the real number of samples returned by DMA and hard-caps at 1024.
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] 2. No per-capture epoch is being set from the actual buffer, so relative timestamps can be wrong on
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] subsequent captures.
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] 3. No filtering on CS state. When the monitored ROM is not selected you get either garbage
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] addresses or bus values from other devices.
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] Suggested next steps
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] - After `snoopComplete()`, trim `pinBuffer` to the actual count returned by
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] `getCapturedSampleCount()`.
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] - Establish `epoch` from the first valid sample of the current capture only.
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] - In the logging loop, only emit entries where CS == 0 (ROM selected) and the access is a
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] read. This will remove the `$0000,00,W` noise and focus on actual ROM traffic.
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] Would you like me to show the updated versions of `snoopRom` and `snoopLog` that do the above (plus
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] a small helper to decode the CS/RW fields cleanly)? I can also add an optional address-range filter
2026-09-09 17:33:04 [grok-build-0.1] if you tell me which ROM region you are currently trying to trace (C000–DFFF or E000–FFFF).
