2026-09-10 09:08:06 [grok-build-0.1] Quick summary of what you sent:
2026-09-10 09:08:06 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-10 09:08:06 [grok-build-0.1] - `scrapeschema.gd` — A small runtime scraper that walks `ClassDB`, buckets classes into
2026-09-10 09:08:06 [grok-build-0.1] `node`/`resource`/`object`/`editor` categories, collects variant types, and writes a JSON schema.
2026-09-10 09:08:06 [grok-build-0.1] - `godotSchema.json.txt` — The output of that scraper (looks like Godot 4.x, with
2026-09-10 09:08:06 [grok-build-0.1] `"latest": "4.7.2"`).
2026-09-10 09:08:06 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-10 09:08:06 [grok-build-0.1] Observations
2026-09-10 09:08:06 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-10 09:08:07 [grok-build-0.1] • Positive:*
2026-09-10 09:08:07 [grok-build-0.1] - The categorization logic is straightforward and uses `ClassDB.is_parent_class()` correctly.
2026-09-10 09:08:07 [grok-build-0.1] - Collecting variants via `type_string()` is a clean way to get the built-in types.
2026-09-10 09:08:07 [grok-build-0.1] - Separating `editor` classes is useful if this schema is intended for tooling or documentation.
2026-09-10 09:08:07 [grok-build-0.1] - The script is self-contained and quits after running, which is appropriate for a generation tool.
2026-09-10 09:08:07 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-10 09:08:07 [grok-build-0.1] • Things worth noting:*
2026-09-10 09:08:07 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-09-10 09:08:07 [grok-build-0.1] 1. Overlapping categories
2026-09-10 09:08:07 [grok-build-0.1]    Many classes appear in both `"node"` (or `"resource"`) and `"object"`. This is expected
2026-09-10 09:08:07 [grok-build-0.1] given the inheritance, but depending on how you intend to consume this schema it may or may not be
2026-09-10 09:08:07 [grok-build-0.1] desirable.
