2026-09-11 08:34:37 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.3 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.007 68.38KB 21.22s
2026-09-11 08:34:37 [deepseek-flash] The shared content is consistent and mostly what I’d expect from a schema-export workflow:
2026-09-11 08:34:37 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-11 08:34:37 [deepseek-flash] - `scrapeschema.gd` is a headless `SceneTree` exporter. It builds the schema dictionary,
2026-09-11 08:34:37 [deepseek-flash] writes JSON, and quits. It exports the category lists (`node`, `resource`, `object`, `editor`), the
2026-09-11 08:34:37 [deepseek-flash] `variant` list, engine version metadata, and repository/license fields.
2026-09-11 08:34:37 [deepseek-flash] - `godotSchema.json.txt` matches that exporter’s structure. The JSON contains no method or
2026-09-11 08:34:37 [deepseek-flash] property signatures, only class-name categories and variants.
2026-09-11 08:34:37 [deepseek-flash] - `scrapegodot.txt` is a related but different script. It extends `Node`, runs from
2026-09-11 08:34:37 [deepseek-flash] `_ready`, and includes signature-formatting helpers (`formatParam`, `formatParamList`,
2026-09-11 08:34:37 [deepseek-flash] `getSignature`, `getSignatures`). Those helpers are not used by `scrapeschema.gd` and are not
2026-09-11 08:34:37 [deepseek-flash] reflected in the JSON.
2026-09-11 08:34:37 [deepseek-flash] - The first file appears to be a base64-encoded copy of `scrapeschema.gd`.
2026-09-11 08:34:37 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-11 08:34:37 [deepseek-flash] Notable differences and observations:
2026-09-11 08:34:37 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-11 08:34:37 [deepseek-flash] - `scrapeschema.gd` is better suited for command-line/headless export because it uses `SceneTree`
2026-09-11 08:34:37 [deepseek-flash] and calls `quit()`.
2026-09-11 08:34:37 [deepseek-flash] - `scrapegodot.txt` has the scaffolding for scraping method signatures, but it does not actually
2026-09-11 08:34:37 [deepseek-flash] write them into the schema.
2026-09-11 08:34:37 [deepseek-flash] - The JSON’s `variant` list is generated from `type_string` and starts at type ID `1`, so it omits
2026-09-11 08:34:37 [deepseek-flash] `Nil` and includes `Variant` manually. That matches the output.
2026-09-11 08:34:37 [deepseek-flash] - The category lists intentionally overlap: a `Node` is also an `Object`, a `Resource` is also an
2026-09-11 08:34:37 [deepseek-flash] `Object`, and editor classes can appear in both `node`/`resource` and `editor`.
2026-09-11 08:34:37 [deepseek-flash] - Both scripts use `ClassDB.is_parent_class(current_name, "Node")` etc. That is the correct Godot 4
2026-09-11 08:34:37 [deepseek-flash] pattern for checking inheritance from the perspective of the current class.
2026-09-11 08:34:37 [deepseek-flash] - Minor style issues: `scrapegodot.txt` has some inconsistent indentation, and `scrapeschema.gd`
2026-09-11 08:34:37 [deepseek-flash] contains a stray string literal near the top that has no effect.
2026-09-11 08:34:37 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-11 08:34:37 [deepseek-flash] If the goal is a richer API schema, the next step would be to merge the signature helpers from
2026-09-11 08:34:37 [deepseek-flash] `scrapegodot.txt` into `build_schema_dictionary()` and add per-class method/property data to the
2026-09-11 08:34:37 [deepseek-flash] JSON. As it stands, the JSON is a class-category schema, not a full API signature dump.
2026-09-11 08:34:38 [deepseek-flash] 
