2026-09-11 14:13:12 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.3 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.005 75.82KB 16.44s
2026-09-11 14:13:12 [deepseek-flash] That constant evaluates to 8,388,608 bytes = 8 MiB exactly (512 KB × 16). If your intent
2026-09-11 14:13:12 [deepseek-flash] was 16 MB, you're off by 2× — `16  1024  1024` is 16,777,216. The expression reads as "512
2026-09-11 14:13:12 [deepseek-flash] KB times 16", which is an easy way to land on 8 MB while thinking 16.
2026-09-11 14:13:12 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-11 14:13:12 [deepseek-flash] That alone wouldn't "fail silently" though — it would just enforce half the ceiling. Silent failure
2026-09-11 14:13:12 [deepseek-flash] usually means something upstream rejects the payload before your constant is ever
2026-09-11 14:13:12 [deepseek-flash] consulted, and the rejection never reaches the client.
2026-09-11 14:13:12 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-11 14:13:12 [deepseek-flash] Likely culprits, in order
2026-09-11 14:13:12 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-11 14:13:12 [deepseek-flash] 1. A reverse proxy or body parser caps first.
2026-09-11 14:13:12 [deepseek-flash]    - nginx `client_max_body_size` defaults to 1 MB → returns 413, your app code never runs.
2026-09-11 14:13:12 [deepseek-flash]    - Caddy `request_body max_size`, Cloudflare (100 MB free tier), or an origin cap.
2026-09-11 14:13:12 [deepseek-flash]    - Express `express.json({ limit })` defaults to 100 KB.
2026-09-11 14:13:12 [deepseek-flash]    If the client doesn't read the HTTP status or WS close code, the drop is invisible.
2026-09-11 14:13:12 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-11 14:13:12 [deepseek-flash] 2. WebSocket `maxPayload`. The `ws` library defaults to 100 MiB, but if it's set lower the
2026-09-11 14:13:12 [deepseek-flash] server closes with code 1009 (message too big) and your size check is never reached.
2026-09-11 14:13:12 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-11 14:13:12 [deepseek-flash] 3. Wrong unit or type at the check. Comparing a byte limit against a JS string's `.length`
2026-09-11 14:13:12 [deepseek-flash] (UTF-16 units) or against base64 text inflates ~1.33×. Use `Buffer.byteLength(...)`.
2026-09-11 14:13:12 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-11 14:13:12 [deepseek-flash] 4. The error is swallowed. An empty `catch {}`, `.catch(() => {})`, or try/finally that
2026-09-11 14:13:12 [deepseek-flash] doesn't rethrow will trigger the limit and tell nobody.
2026-09-11 14:13:12 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-11 14:13:12 [deepseek-flash] 5. Two sources of truth. If the relay reads its limit from config or an env var, editing
2026-09-11 14:13:12 [deepseek-flash] this constant changes nothing — the config wins silently.
2026-09-11 14:13:12 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-11 14:13:12 [deepseek-flash] 6. Truncation instead of rejection. Some buffers/protocols silently truncate rather than
2026-09-11 14:13:12 [deepseek-flash] error.
2026-09-11 14:13:12 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-11 14:13:13 [deepseek-flash] Quick diagnostics
2026-09-11 14:13:13 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-11 14:13:13 [deepseek-flash] - Log at the check site and at the HTTP/WS layer, then upload a file just under and just
2026-09-11 14:13:13 [deepseek-flash] over 8 MiB.
2026-09-11 14:13:13 [deepseek-flash] - Watch for 413 on the HTTP path and close code 1009 on the WS path — those tell
2026-09-11 14:13:13 [deepseek-flash] you which layer is cutting it.
2026-09-11 14:13:13 [deepseek-flash] - Confirm `Buffer.byteLength(payload)` vs `payload.length` if the body is text or base64.
2026-09-11 14:13:13 [deepseek-flash] - Check for a second hardcoded limit or a config/env override.
2026-09-11 14:13:13 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-11 14:13:13 [deepseek-flash] To narrow it down: what's the relay stack — Node + `ws`, Nostr, or something else — and is it
2026-09-11 14:13:13 [deepseek-flash] behind nginx, Caddy, or Cloudflare? That determines which of the above is actually biting.
2026-09-11 14:13:13 [deepseek-flash] 
