2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] Good — that log line changes the diagnosis. This is your own guard doing its job, not a
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] proxy or transport limit. The earlier nginx/`ws maxPayload` theories don't apply here, because the
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] path is a local Windows file (`C:\nitrologic\relay\gd\extension_api.json`) — the relay is reading
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] it off disk and your constant is the thing that stopped it.
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] What's actually happening
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] `extension_api.json` is Godot's own generated API dump, produced by `godot --headless
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] --dump-extension-api`. It's a large single JSON document — several MB for Godot 4.x, and it grows
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] with every release as classes are added. Your cap is 8 MiB (`512102416`), and the
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] file exceeds it, so the ingest aborts.
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] So the failure was never silent at the check — it was silent at the surface. The guard
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] logged and bailed, but nothing told the caller. That's the real bug to fix, separate from the size
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] question.
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] The arithmetic, again
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] 512 * 1024 * 16  =  8,388,608 bytes  =  8 MiB
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] 16 * 1024 * 1024 = 16,777,216 bytes  = 16 MiB
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] If you meant 16 MB, you're enforcing half of it. Worth expressing the constant directly in MiB
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] rather than as "512 KB × N" — the multiplication hides the intent and invites exactly this.
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] Options
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] ╭─────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] │  Option                 │  Tradeoff                                                                                    │
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] ├─────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] │  Raise the cap          │  Simplest. But pick a number you can defend, not just "bigger than this file."               │
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] │  Compress before relay  │  JSON gzips ~10×. Best if the wire is the constraint.                                        │
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] │  Don't relay this file  │  It's a regenerable build artifact — the consumer could run `--dump-extension-api` locally.  │
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] │  Chunk / stream it      │  Most work; only worth it if you expect files of this class routinely.                       │
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] ╰─────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] My instinct: if this file is the only thing tripping the cap, option 3 is the cleanest —
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] you're shipping a build output through a relay that otherwise handles smaller payloads. If several
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] files of this size are expected, raise the cap and gzip.
2026-09-11 14:13:34 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-11 14:13:35 [deepseek-flash] Before deciding
2026-09-11 14:13:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-11 14:13:35 [deepseek-flash] Check the actual size on disk — `dir` in that folder, or right-click → Properties. That tells you
2026-09-11 14:13:35 [deepseek-flash] whether 16 MiB clears it comfortably or whether you need 32, and it's the number you should size
2026-09-11 14:13:35 [deepseek-flash] the cap against.
2026-09-11 14:13:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-11 14:13:35 [deepseek-flash] Also worth confirming: is `extension_api.json` something the relay needs to carry, or did
2026-09-11 14:13:35 [deepseek-flash] it get swept in by a directory walk? The `gd\` subfolder suggests it may be incidental to whatever
2026-09-11 14:13:35 [deepseek-flash] you were actually relaying.
2026-09-11 14:13:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-11 14:13:35 [deepseek-flash] Separate fix
2026-09-11 14:13:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-11 14:13:35 [deepseek-flash] Whatever you decide on the limit, make the rejection visible — return an error to the caller rather
2026-09-11 14:13:35 [deepseek-flash] than only logging it. A guard that stops work and tells nobody is how you ended up reading the log
2026-09-11 14:13:35 [deepseek-flash] to find this.
2026-09-11 14:13:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-11 14:13:35 [deepseek-flash] What's the relay meant to carry — is `gd\` a deliberate payload set, or a folder it walked into?
2026-09-11 14:13:35 [deepseek-flash] 
