2026-09-15 08:47:43 [kimi-k2.7-code] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.3 kimi-k2.7-code 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.002 489B 7.04s
2026-09-15 08:47:43 [kimi-k2.7-code] Short answer: not strictly required, but you should use one, especially if your
2026-09-15 08:47:43 [kimi-k2.7-code] machine is directly exposed to the internet or runs services.
2026-09-15 08:47:43 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-15 08:47:43 [kimi-k2.7-code] Arch Linux doesn’t ship with a firewall enabled by default. On a typical home desktop behind a
2026-09-15 08:47:43 [kimi-k2.7-code] router/NAT, the risk is lower, but a firewall still gives you a useful “deny by default” safety net
2026-09-15 08:47:43 [kimi-k2.7-code] if you install something that opens a port, or if you connect to untrusted networks.
2026-09-15 08:47:43 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-15 08:47:43 [kimi-k2.7-code] If you run a server, SSH, torrent software, a game server, etc., treat a firewall as essential.
2026-09-15 08:47:43 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-15 08:47:43 [kimi-k2.7-code] Common choices on Arch
2026-09-15 08:47:43 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-15 08:47:43 [kimi-k2.7-code] - nftables — modern, default in the kernel; good if you want to write rules directly.
2026-09-15 08:47:43 [kimi-k2.7-code] - iptables — older, still widely used, but nftables is the current direction.
2026-09-15 08:47:43 [kimi-k2.7-code] - firewalld — dynamic firewall with zones; easy if you want a GUI or simple CLI.
2026-09-15 08:47:43 [kimi-k2.7-code] - ufw — simpler frontend for iptables/nftables; beginner-friendly.
2026-09-15 08:47:43 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-15 08:47:43 [kimi-k2.7-code] Quick starting point
2026-09-15 08:47:43 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-15 08:47:43 [kimi-k2.7-code] For a simple workstation, `firewalld` or `ufw` is usually enough:
2026-09-15 08:47:43 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-15 08:47:43 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-15 08:47:43 [kimi-k2.7-code] sudo pacman -S firewalld
2026-09-15 08:47:43 [kimi-k2.7-code] sudo systemctl enable --now firewalld
2026-09-15 08:47:43 [kimi-k2.7-code] sudo firewall-cmd --set-default-zone=home
2026-09-15 08:47:43 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-15 08:47:43 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-15 08:47:44 [kimi-k2.7-code] or with `ufw`:
2026-09-15 08:47:44 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-15 08:47:44 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-15 08:47:44 [kimi-k2.7-code] sudo pacman -S ufw
2026-09-15 08:47:44 [kimi-k2.7-code] sudo ufw default deny incoming
2026-09-15 08:47:44 [kimi-k2.7-code] sudo ufw default allow outgoing
2026-09-15 08:47:44 [kimi-k2.7-code] sudo ufw enable
2026-09-15 08:47:44 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-15 08:47:44 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-15 08:47:44 [kimi-k2.7-code] Useful Arch Wiki pages
2026-09-15 08:47:44 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-15 08:47:44 [kimi-k2.7-code] - [Firewalls](https://wiki.archlinux.org/title/Firewalls)
2026-09-15 08:47:44 [kimi-k2.7-code] - [nftables](https://wiki.archlinux.org/title/Nftables)
2026-09-15 08:47:44 [kimi-k2.7-code] - [Firewalld](https://wiki.archlinux.org/title/Firewalld)
2026-09-15 08:47:44 [kimi-k2.7-code] - [Ufw](https://wiki.archlinux.org/title/Uncomplicated_Firewall)
2026-09-15 08:47:44 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-15 08:47:44 [kimi-k2.7-code] If you tell me whether this is a desktop or server and what services you’re running, I can suggest
2026-09-15 08:47:44 [kimi-k2.7-code] a more specific setup.
