2026-09-15 09:11:20 [kimi-k2.7-code] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.3 kimi-k2.7-code 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.003 2.344KB 7.78s
2026-09-15 09:11:20 [kimi-k2.7-code] For an internet-facing server running nginx, yes, use a firewall. At minimum you want to
2026-09-15 09:11:20 [kimi-k2.7-code] allow only SSH, HTTP, and HTTPS, and drop everything else.
2026-09-15 09:11:20 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-15 09:11:20 [kimi-k2.7-code] The cleanest choice on Arch is nftables — it’s the modern standard, installed by default
2026-09-15 09:11:20 [kimi-k2.7-code] with the kernel, and has no extra dependencies.
2026-09-15 09:11:20 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-15 09:11:20 [kimi-k2.7-code] Important warning
2026-09-15 09:11:20 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-15 09:11:20 [kimi-k2.7-code] If you’re doing this over SSH, make sure you allow SSH first and keep your current
2026-09-15 09:11:20 [kimi-k2.7-code] session open until you confirm you can reconnect. Otherwise you can lock yourself out.
2026-09-15 09:11:20 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-15 09:11:20 [kimi-k2.7-code] Minimal nftables setup for nginx
2026-09-15 09:11:20 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-15 09:11:20 [kimi-k2.7-code] Create `/etc/nftables.conf`:
2026-09-15 09:11:20 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-15 09:11:20 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-15 09:11:20 [kimi-k2.7-code] #!/usr/bin/nft -f
2026-09-15 09:11:20 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-15 09:11:20 [kimi-k2.7-code] flush ruleset
2026-09-15 09:11:21 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-09-15 09:11:21 [kimi-k2.7-code] table inet filter {
2026-09-15 09:11:21 [kimi-k2.7-code] 	chain input {
2026-09-15 09:11:21 [kimi-k2.7-code] 		type filter hook input priority 0; policy drop;
2026-09-15 09:11:21 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
