2026-09-15 13:42:04 [deepseek-flash] A few observations on this one:
2026-09-15 13:42:04 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-15 13:42:04 [deepseek-flash] • What's accurate and well-chosen*
2026-09-15 13:42:04 [deepseek-flash] - Highland Park, Illinois is correct, and the peers list is defensible: Lessig was a mentor and
2026-09-15 13:42:04 [deepseek-flash] Creative Commons collaborator; Berners-Lee connects through the W3C RDF Core Working Group and the
2026-09-15 13:42:04 [deepseek-flash] Semantic Web work.
2026-09-15 13:42:04 [deepseek-flash] - The two ethos topics are a good compression of his arc — RSS 1.0 is protocol design, and the
2026-09-15 13:42:04 [deepseek-flash] JSTOR/Open Access work is information access. They're the through-line rather than just a list of
2026-09-15 13:42:04 [deepseek-flash] projects.
2026-09-15 13:42:04 [deepseek-flash] - Dates check out: 8 November 1986, 11 January 2013.
2026-09-15 13:42:04 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-15 13:42:04 [deepseek-flash] • What's new or different here (versus a typical bio blurb)*
2026-09-15 13:42:04 [deepseek-flash] - Structuring the person as `ethos` + `journey` rather than a prose paragraph is the interesting
2026-09-15 13:42:04 [deepseek-flash] move — it forces the biography to declare a thesis rather than recite a résumé.
2026-09-15 13:42:04 [deepseek-flash] - `credits` as an array with a model listed alongside a human author is notable metadata. It's
2026-09-15 13:42:05 [deepseek-flash] honest, but it also means the file format itself has to be able to express "who made this" when the
2026-09-15 13:42:05 [deepseek-flash] answer is mixed.
2026-09-15 13:42:05 [deepseek-flash] - The description ("knee deep in RSS and open systems") is deliberately informal. Given the rest of
2026-09-15 13:42:05 [deepseek-flash] the schema is tidy and structured, the tone reads as a deliberate counterweight rather than an
2026-09-15 13:42:05 [deepseek-flash] oversight — though it's the one line that would look out of place if this file is ever rendered
2026-09-15 13:42:05 [deepseek-flash] next to, say, a Kant entry.
2026-09-15 13:42:05 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-15 13:42:05 [deepseek-flash] • Things I'd flag*
2026-09-15 13:42:05 [deepseek-flash] - MIT is a software license. Applying it to a biographical record is unusual — worth deciding
2026-09-15 13:42:05 [deepseek-flash] whether `license` here means the file, the schema, or the content, because
2026-09-15 13:42:05 [deepseek-flash] those have different answers and a reader may assume the wrong one.
2026-09-15 13:42:05 [deepseek-flash] - `version: 0.1` plus a single copyright year suggests this schema is early. If entries will
2026-09-15 13:42:05 [deepseek-flash] eventually be cross-referenced by `peers`, those strings will need to resolve to other files
2026-09-15 13:42:05 [deepseek-flash] (Berners-Lee, Lessig) — otherwise they're just text and the graph doesn't close.
2026-09-15 13:42:05 [deepseek-flash] - Minor: `topics` has a trailing space after `"Protocol Design", ` — harmless, but if anything
