2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] different ones.
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] • 1. Resolving a bare name to an adjacent resource (the mechanism)*
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] - RFC 3986 §5 — relative-reference resolution against a base URI. Everything below is a
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] specialisation of this.
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] - JSON Schema `id` / `ref` — a subschema's `id` establishes the base for nested `ref`s, so
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] sibling files resolve by position. `dynamicAnchor`/`dynamicRef` (draft 2020‑12) is the one
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] mechanism designed specifically for "resolve to whichever sibling overrides this."
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] - JSON-LD `@base` / `@id` — relative IRIs in `@id` expand against the document base.
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] RO‑Crate is the worked example: entities are `./biography/aaronswartz.json`, resolved
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] against `ro-crate-metadata.json`.
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] - Bazel labels — `//books/biography:timbernerslee`, with `:name` meaning "in this package."
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] Package-relative naming is the whole point of the syntax. Buck2 and Pants share it.
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] - PSR‑4 (PHP), Java packages, C# namespaces, PEP 328 relative imports, Rust `super::` —
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] namespace/name determines path by convention. PSR‑4 is the cleanest statement of the rule.
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] - C `#include "x.h"` vs `<x.h>` — the crisp framing of the design choice: quote-relative
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] (adjacent) versus search-path (authority-resolved).
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] - MediaWiki wikilinks — title → adjacent page, with namespace and redirect resolution. The
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] oldest widely-deployed version of this.
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] - WebFinger (RFC 7033) and `.well-known` (RFC 8615) — discovery when there is no
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] path at all. AT Protocol's handle → DID resolution goes through `/.well-known/atproto-did` for
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] exactly this reason.
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] • 2. Typing the relation (what kind of peer)*
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] - RFC 8288 Web Linking — the IANA registry includes `related`, `prev`, `next`, `alternate`,
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] `via`, `author`, `item`. Atom (RFC 4287) uses the same registry.
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] - FOAF `foaf:knows` — the original "peers" property, and the FOAF convention is that the
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] document lives at a dereferenceable adjacent URL.
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] - schema.org `knows`, `follows`, `memberOf`, `sameAs` — note there is no `colleague`
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] property; that one only exists in vCard.
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] - vCard 4 (RFC 6350) `RELATED;TYPE=colleague` — a formal peer-type vocabulary.
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] - Dublin Core `dcterms:relation` / `references` / `isReferencedBy` / `replaces` — the
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] generic library answer.
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] - OWL `sameAs`, RDFS `seeAlso` / `isDefinedBy` — `isDefinedBy` is literally "the adjacent
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] document that defines this."
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] • 3. The bibliographic precedents (closest to your case)*
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] These are the ones worth reading before choosing, because they've already fought the name-collision
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] problem:
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] - BibLaTeX `related` / `relatedtype` — a key references another key in the same `.bib`,
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] with a typed relation (`reviewof`, `bytranslator`, `multivolume`). This is the nearest structural
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] analogue to `peers`.
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] - BibTeX `crossref` and BibLaTeX `@xdata` — inheritance between adjacent entries.
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] - Crossref relation types — `is-related-to`, `is-preprint-of`, `is-supplement-to`,
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] `is-reply-to`, `is-version-of`.
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] - DataCite `relationType` — `IsRelatedTo`, `IsCitedBy`, `IsPartOf`, `Continues`,
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] `IsDerivedFrom`.
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] - MARC 787 (nonspecific relationship) plus 780/785 (preceding/succeeding), with `$w` for
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] the link. MARC 7XX is the "added entry" family — peers, in library terms.
2026-09-15 13:47:03 [deepseek-flash] - MADS `<related>` between authority records; BIBFRAME relation properties
2026-09-15 13:47:04 [deepseek-flash] (`precededBy`, `succeededBy`, `references`/`referencedBy`).
2026-09-15 13:47:04 [deepseek-flash] - RDA Appendix K — relationship designators between persons: collaborator, associate,
2026-09-15 13:47:04 [deepseek-flash] friend, spouse.
2026-09-15 13:47:04 [deepseek-flash] - TEI `<listRelation>` / `<relation name="peer" mutual="true">` — peer relations as
2026-09-15 13:47:04 [deepseek-flash] first-class, bidirectional.
2026-09-15 13:47:04 [deepseek-flash] - METS `xlink:href` and OAI‑ORE resource maps — relative links between files in a
2026-09-15 13:47:04 [deepseek-flash] package.
2026-09-15 13:47:04 [deepseek-flash] - ISAAR(CPF) 5.3 and ISAD(G) 3.5.3 — the archival equivalents.
2026-09-15 13:47:04 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-15 13:47:04 [deepseek-flash] • What this means for your two options*
2026-09-15 13:47:04 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-15 13:47:04 [deepseek-flash] If `peers` stays a plain string, you get nothing for free — every consumer invents its own slugging
2026-09-15 13:47:04 [deepseek-flash] rule, and "Tim Berners-Lee" → `timbernerslee` vs `tim-berners-lee` vs `bern-ers-lee` is a silent
2026-09-15 13:47:04 [deepseek-flash] failure. Name-slugging is also locale-hostile: "Türkiye", "Côte d'Ivoire", "São Paulo" all break
2026-09-15 13:47:04 [deepseek-flash] naive rules.
2026-09-15 13:47:04 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-15 13:47:04 [deepseek-flash] If you want adjacency, the closest fit to what you already have is JSON-LD with `@base` set to
2026-09-15 13:47:04 [deepseek-flash] `books/biography/`, so `@id: "timbernerslee.json"` resolves by position, with the relation typed
2026-09-15 13:47:04 [deepseek-flash] via RFC 8288's `related`. That is RO‑Crate's model almost exactly.
2026-09-15 13:47:04 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-15 13:47:04 [deepseek-flash] But note you already carry the better key. Your `identifier` block in `biblispec.json` has VIAF,
2026-09-15 13:47:04 [deepseek-flash] ISNI, Wikidata, ORCID. Those are stable, unique, and locale-independent — the reason authority
2026-09-15 13:47:04 [deepseek-flash] files exist. A `peers` entry that is a Wikidata Q‑number sidesteps slugging entirely and lets the
2026-09-15 13:47:04 [deepseek-flash] path be derived rather than guessed. The cost is that it stops being human-readable, which may be
2026-09-15 13:47:04 [deepseek-flash] why you wrote plain names in the first place.
2026-09-15 13:47:04 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-15 13:47:04 [deepseek-flash] The honest tradeoff: readable names are lossy keys, stable IDs are opaque ones. Standards that try
2026-09-15 13:47:04 [deepseek-flash] to have both — DID Core's `alsoKnownAs`, ActivityPub's `alsoKnownAs`/`movedTo`, IndieWeb `rel=me` —
2026-09-15 13:47:04 [deepseek-flash] all end up carrying both a human handle and a machine identifier, and accepting that they can drift
2026-09-15 13:47:04 [deepseek-flash] apart.
2026-09-15 13:47:04 [deepseek-flash] 
