2026-09-15 14:05:44 [nitro@ryzen5] lifetime of connection has been fine until arch linux as host, interested in more why than how to fix
2026-09-15 14:06:25 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.3 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.009 564B 40.02s
2026-09-15 14:06:25 [deepseek-flash] Assuming you mean SSH/TCP sessions to an Arch host, the short version is: Arch didn’t break
2026-09-15 14:06:25 [deepseek-flash] connection lifetime; it removed the distro-specific defaults that were hiding the real timeout.
2026-09-15 14:06:25 [deepseek-flash] A connection stays alive only as long as every layer in the path agrees it’s alive. The effective
2026-09-15 14:06:25 [deepseek-flash] lifetime is the minimum of the application keepalive, TCP keepalive, firewall/conntrack
2026-09-15 14:06:25 [deepseek-flash] timeout, NAT timeout, Wi‑Fi power‑save behaviour, and power management. Change the host OS, and you
2026-09-15 14:06:25 [deepseek-flash] change the defaults in that chain.
2026-09-15 14:06:25 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-15 14:06:25 [deepseek-flash] Arch is minimal, rolling, and close to upstream. It generally does not ship the tuning
2026-09-15 14:06:25 [deepseek-flash] files that Debian/Ubuntu/Fedora may add. So you get upstream kernel and package defaults. If your
2026-09-15 14:06:25 [deepseek-flash] previous host had a config that sent keepalives, disabled Wi‑Fi powersave, or set a firewall
2026-09-15 14:06:25 [deepseek-flash] timeout, Arch won’t have it unless you add it.
2026-09-15 14:06:25 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-15 14:06:25 [deepseek-flash] The usual Arch‑specific suspects:
2026-09-15 14:06:25 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] 1. sshd keepalives
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash]    Arch’s `sshd_config` is upstream: `ClientAliveInterval 0`, `TCPKeepAlive yes`. TCP keepalive on
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] Linux defaults to `net.ipv4.tcp_keepalive_time = 7200` (2 hours). Many NATs drop idle TCP after
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] 5–60 minutes. So the connection is already dead from the middlebox’s point of view, but the host
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] won’t notice for up to two hours. Other distros or cloud images often set `ClientAliveInterval`;
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] Arch leaves it commented out.
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] 2. Wi‑Fi power save
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash]    NetworkManager’s default is `wifi.powersave=3` (enable). Arch doesn’t override it. Some distros
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] ship a `/etc/NetworkManager/conf.d/` file to disable it. This is a very common cause of idle drops,
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] especially on Intel Wi‑Fi.
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] 3. Firewall / conntrack
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash]    Arch has no firewall by default, but if you install one, its conntrack timeouts may be shorter
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] than the kernel’s 5‑day TCP established timeout. Also, Arch’s `iptables` is nft‑based, which can
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] behave differently from legacy iptables on older distros.
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] 4. systemd-logind
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash]    `IdleAction`, `KillUserProcesses`, and session timeouts can kill sessions. Arch’s systemd is
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] newer, and defaults are usually safe, but if you enabled something it can terminate idle sessions.
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] 5. Power management
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash]    TLP, laptop‑mode, suspend on idle or lid close. Arch users often install TLP; other distros may
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] not have it. Suspending the host kills connections.
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] 6. Newer kernel and drivers
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash]    Rolling release means new TCP stack, NIC firmware, and Wi‑Fi drivers. A regression or changed
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] default (IPv6, MTU probing, etc.) can affect long‑lived connections. If it started after `pacman
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] -Syu`, check `dmesg`.
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] 7. NetworkManager / dhcpcd / systemd-networkd
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash]    DHCP lease renewal or IPv6 RA lifetimes can cause address changes and drop connections.
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] Different distros use different network stacks and defaults.
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] Why other distros seem fine: they often ship `/usr/lib/sysctl.d/` or `/etc/NetworkManager/conf.d/`
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] files that lower keepalive time, disable Wi‑Fi powersave, or set SSH keepalives. Arch leaves these
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] to you. So the “lifetime” you saw before was likely a distro‑specific default, not a property of
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] Linux itself.
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] If you want to confirm which layer is timing out, look at:
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] - `sysctl net.ipv4.tcp_keepalive_time net.ipv4.tcp_keepalive_intvl net.ipv4.tcp_keepalive_probes`
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] - `ss -o state established` to see the keepalive timer
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] - `iw dev <iface> get power_save` and `nmcli -f 802-11-wireless.powersave connection show <conn>`
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] - `nft list ruleset` and `conntrack -L`
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] - `journalctl -u sshd -u NetworkManager -u systemd-logind -b`
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] - `dmesg | grep -i -E 'iwlwifi|firmware|link is not ready'`
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] That will show whether the host is sending keepalives, whether powersave is on, or whether
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] conntrack is dropping. The fix is usually to align the keepalive at the layer that times out first
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] — but the why is that Arch exposes the upstream timeout that your previous distro was
2026-09-15 14:06:26 [deepseek-flash] silently overriding.
