2026-09-15 14:45:22 [deepseek-flash] • One of the two `substr(npos)` sites was fixed.* The 404 branch now guards `r ==
2026-09-15 14:45:23 [deepseek-flash] std::string::npos`. The second one, in the `loadString` success path, is unchanged: `size_t
2026-09-15 14:45:23 [deepseek-flash] r=filepath.rfind('.'); utf8 suffix=filepath.substr(r);`. If a request resolves to an existing file
2026-09-15 14:45:23 [deepseek-flash] with no dot in the name, that throws `std::out_of_range`, which is uncaught and terminates. Same
2026-09-15 14:45:23 [deepseek-flash] one-line guard applies.
2026-09-15 14:45:23 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-15 14:45:23 [deepseek-flash] • `closeHandle()` still has no `return`. `int closeHandle()` falls off the end. At
2026-09-15 14:45:23 [deepseek-flash] `-O0` the caller reads whatever is in `eax`; harmless here only because the return value is
2026-09-15 14:45:23 [deepseek-flash] discarded. `-w` in `CMakeLists.txt` is suppressing the warning that would flag it, along with the
2026-09-15 14:45:23 [deepseek-flash] uninitialised members I mentioned before (`EventSink::eventCounter`, `AudioBuffer::total`, the
2026-09-15 14:45:23 [deepseek-flash] `udev udev` in `listPorts`).
2026-09-15 14:45:23 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-15 14:45:23 [deepseek-flash] • The `httpTask` reorder* — moving `closeHandle()` inside the mutex block — doesn't change
2026-09-15 14:45:23 [deepseek-flash] correctness either way. It just holds the lock across a `close(2)`. The shutdown path in
2026-09-15 14:45:23 [deepseek-flash] `closeHttpTasks` still works because closing a blocked thread's fd makes its `recv` return and it
2026-09-15 14:45:23 [deepseek-flash] then queues on the same mutex.
2026-09-15 14:45:23 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-15 14:45:23 [deepseek-flash] Also worth noting: when the new guard rejects an oversize body, control falls through to
2026-09-15 14:45:23 [deepseek-flash] `if(content.length()!=contentLength) return false;`, which logs "content length does not match" and
2026-09-15 14:45:23 [deepseek-flash] drops the connection without a response. A 413 would be more accurate, and would stop the client
2026-09-15 14:45:23 [deepseek-flash] hanging until the receive timeout.
2026-09-15 14:45:23 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-15 14:45:23 [deepseek-flash] If the SIGILL survives this, the next step is still the raw bytes at `pc` — `x/8xb pc` in the
2026-09-15 14:45:23 [deepseek-flash] frame. `0f 0b` confirms a compiler or libstdc++ trap; anything else means the instruction pointer
2026-09-15 14:45:23 [deepseek-flash] is misattributed and the real fault is a corrupted return address on the stack, which is a
2026-09-15 14:45:23 [deepseek-flash] different investigation.
2026-09-15 14:45:23 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:24:37 [nitro@ryzen5] use rsync to copy newer files from 1 path to another recursively
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.000 527B 1.80s
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] Use `rsync` with `-r` (recursive) and `-u` (update, skip files that are newer on the destination):
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] rsync -ru /source/path/ /destination/path/
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] Key points:
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] - Trailing slash on source matters. `/source/path/` means "copy the contents of path";
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] `/source/path` means "copy the path directory itself into the destination".
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] - `-u` skips any file on the destination that is newer than the source. It does not delete
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] or overwrite newer destination files.
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] - Add `-v` for verbose output and `-n` for a dry run to preview what would be transferred:
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] rsync -ruvn /source/path/ /destination/path/
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] - If you also want to preserve timestamps, permissions, and recurse cleanly:
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] rsync -ruav /source/path/ /destination/path/
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] - For remote copies:
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] rsync -ruav /local/path/ user@host:/remote/path/
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] rsync -ruav user@host:/remote/path/ /local/path/
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] Note: `-u` compares mtime only. If you want rsync's normal "transfer only changed files" behaviour
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] (based on size + mtime, regardless of which is newer), drop `-u`:
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] rsync -rav /source/path/ /destination/path/
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:24:41 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:25:44 [nitro@ryzen5] can it log for each new copy
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.000 1.868KB 2.28s
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] Yes. `rsync` has `--out-format` (or the older `-i` / `--itemize-changes`) for per-file logging, and
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] you can write that to a file with `--log-file`.
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] • Log each transferred file to stdout:*
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] rsync -ruav --out-format='%t %i %n' /source/path/ /destination/path/
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] - `%t` — timestamp
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] - `%i` — itemized change flags (e.g. `>f+++++++++` for a new file)
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] - `%n` — filename
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] • Log to a file instead:*
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] rsync -ruav --log-file=/var/log/rsync-copy.log --out-format='%t %i %n' /source/path/ /destination/path/
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] With `--log-file`, rsync writes its own transfer lines to that file. By default it only logs files
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] that are actually transferred (new or updated), which is what you want.
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] • Only show newly created files (not updates):*
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] `--out-format` prints every change. To filter to new files only, you can post-process:
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] rsync -ruav --out-format='%i %n' /source/path/ /destination/path/ \
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] 	| grep '^>f+++++++++'
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] • Useful `%i` flags for interpretation:*
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] - `>f+++++++++` — new file created on destination
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] - `>f.st......` — file updated (size/time changed)
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] - `.d..t......` — directory timestamp changed only
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] • Quieter alternative* if you just want the names of transferred files:
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] rsync -rua --info=name /source/path/ /destination/path/
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] That prints one line per transferred file with no extra chatter.
2026-09-24 11:25:47 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:26:37 [nitro@ryzen5] rsync -rua /mnt/fast/master /mnt/grid26/master
2026-09-24 11:26:42 [nitro@ryzen5] is running will see
2026-09-24 11:26:44 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.000 3.500KB 1.94s
2026-09-24 11:26:44 [deepseek-flash] Worth checking one thing while it runs: with no trailing slash on the source, rsync treats
2026-09-24 11:26:44 [deepseek-flash] `/mnt/fast/master` as a directory to place inside the destination.
2026-09-24 11:26:44 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:26:44 [deepseek-flash] - If `/mnt/grid26/master` does not exist → it creates it and the contents land directly in
2026-09-24 11:26:44 [deepseek-flash] `/mnt/grid26/master/...`. Fine.
2026-09-24 11:26:44 [deepseek-flash] - If `/mnt/grid26/master` already exists → you get `/mnt/grid26/master/master/...` (a
2026-09-24 11:26:44 [deepseek-flash] nested copy).
2026-09-24 11:26:44 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:26:44 [deepseek-flash] You can confirm which happened with:
2026-09-24 11:26:44 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:26:44 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:26:44 [deepseek-flash] ls -la /mnt/grid26/master/
2026-09-24 11:26:44 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:26:44 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:26:44 [deepseek-flash] If you see a `master` directory inside it, that's the nesting case. To get the contents copied
2026-09-24 11:26:44 [deepseek-flash] directly instead, the source needs a trailing slash:
2026-09-24 11:26:44 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:26:44 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:26:44 [deepseek-flash] rsync -rua /mnt/fast/master/ /mnt/grid26/master/
2026-09-24 11:26:44 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:26:44 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-24 11:26:44 [deepseek-flash] Also note `-u` skips files that are newer on the destination, which is what you asked for, but it
2026-09-24 11:26:44 [deepseek-flash] won't delete anything and won't overwrite a newer destination file. If the two trees are meant to
2026-09-24 11:26:44 [deepseek-flash] stay identical, `-a` plus `--delete` (and dropping `-u`) is the usual choice — but that's
2026-09-24 11:26:44 [deepseek-flash] destructive, so only if that's actually your intent.
2026-09-24 11:26:44 [deepseek-flash] 
